BANK.push(
{"id":"n1","d":3,"q":"A food processing plant wants its historian data in a cloud analytics platform. The control network has no IT connection today. Last year a firewall rule error briefly exposed a PLC programming port, so the OT manager says the no-inbound requirement must not depend on rule correctness. The analytics vendor also offers a remote firmware update feature that needs a return path. The plant has a modest budget and a two-person OT team. Which design BEST meets the requirement?","o":["Replicate the historian through a unidirectional gateway to a replica historian in an industrial DMZ, and handle vendor updates through a separate, manually enabled path","Place the historian in an industrial DMZ between two firewalls from different vendors, with the OT-side firewall permitting only sessions initiated from the control network","Install an edge gateway in the control network that publishes to the cloud broker over MQTT with mutual TLS, using an outbound-only connection on port 8883","Let the cloud platform pull data through an IPsec tunnel that terminates on the DMZ firewall, with policy limited to the historian's OPC UA port"],"a":[0],"e":"The deciding fact is that the control must not rely on rule correctness. A unidirectional gateway enforces one-way flow in hardware, and the replica historian gives the cloud something to read. Dual-vendor firewalls and IPsec pull designs still rest on configured rules. An outbound MQTT session is bidirectional at the transport layer, so the broker can send data back into the control network."},
{"id":"n2","d":3,"q":"A firm is replacing its full-tunnel VPN, which gives remote users broad network access. In a recent incident a contractor laptop that passed every posture check was used to scan the network and reach domain controllers. Most apps are web based, but a legacy ERP thick client uses dynamic RPC ports. Leadership wants the VPN retired within six months, and the help desk is short staffed. Which design gives the GREATEST reduction in lateral movement risk?","o":["Keep the VPN but assign contractors to a dedicated address pool and apply firewall policy to that pool limiting them to approved subnets and ports","Broker per-application access with ZTNA, and scope the ERP client to its specific servers and port range instead of granting subnet access","Add stricter posture checks on VPN connect, quarantining devices that lack current EDR signatures or disk encryption","Move the VPN to certificate-based device authentication with AES-256 and require MFA on each reconnect"],"a":[1],"e":"Per-application brokering means a compromised device can only reach the apps it is entitled to, and scoping the ERP to known servers handles the legacy client. Pool-based firewall rules still give network-level reach and keep the VPN. The compromised laptop passed posture, so stricter posture would not have stopped it. Stronger authentication and ciphers do not limit what an authenticated device can reach."},
{"id":"n3","d":3,"q":"A SaaS company encrypts about 4 PB of customer objects with envelope encryption: a unique AES-256 DEK per object, wrapped by a KEK held in an HSM-backed cloud KMS, with the wrapped DEK stored in object metadata. Annual automatic KEK rotation is on. An investigation finds a departed engineer had decrypt rights on the KEK and called Decrypt on about 40,000 wrapped DEKs for one customer's bucket, logging the results. The customer renews next quarter. What is the BEST remediation for that customer's data?","o":["Trigger an immediate KEK rotation, re-wrap every DEK in the account under the new key version, and disable the prior key version in the KMS","Generate new DEKs for the affected objects, re-encrypt those objects, and destroy the old wrapped DEKs after removing the role binding","Revoke the engineer's remaining access, review KMS audit logs for other callers, and require dual approval for Decrypt grants","Move the customer to a dedicated KEK held in a single-tenant HSM, with key use requiring the customer's approval going forward"],"a":[1],"e":"The engineer holds plaintext DEKs. Rotating or disabling the KEK only re-wraps those same DEKs, so the exposed keys still decrypt the data. Only new DEKs and re-encryption contain the exposure. Access review and dual control are needed but do not fix the exposed keys. A dedicated KEK helps in the future but also leaves the old DEKs valid."},
{"id":"n4","d":3,"q":"A pharma company's crypto working group must pick the first flow to move to hybrid post-quantum key exchange. The CISO's concern is adversaries recording traffic now to decrypt later. The board also asked about a phishing campaign against finance staff last month. Four candidate flows are listed. Which flow should be prioritized?","o":["Firmware images signed with ECDSA P-256 and pushed to lab instruments that stay in service in the field for about ten years","Card payments over TLS 1.3 with X25519, where card numbers are tokenized and tokens expire within a day","Clinical trial data sent to a research partner over TLS 1.2 with ECDHE, which must stay confidential for 25 years","Archived backups encrypted with AES-256-GCM, with data keys wrapped by a KMS key that also uses AES-256"],"a":[2],"e":"Harvest now, decrypt later targets recorded key exchanges protecting data that stays sensitive past the arrival of a relevant quantum computer. ECDHE is breakable by Shor's algorithm and the data needs 25 years of secrecy. Signatures face a forgery risk, not recorded-traffic decryption. Tokenized short-lived payment data loses value quickly. AES-256 keeps adequate margin against Grover's algorithm. The phishing detail is noise."},
{"id":"n5","d":3,"q":"An architect is planning post-quantum migration for internet-facing TLS services. Traffic includes current browsers, mobile apps on a two-year update cycle, and a few partner integrations on older runtimes. A compliance lead wants to switch to post-quantum-only key exchange and post-quantum certificates this year to 'get it done once.' Which plan is MOST appropriate?","o":["Issue post-quantum certificates first, since signatures protect long-term identity, and add post-quantum key exchange next year","Enable hybrid classical plus post-quantum key exchange now with fallback, and move certificates as client support matures","Require post-quantum-only key exchange now, falling back to classical only for partners who formally request an exception","Move to the largest classical key sizes and strongest classical ciphers available now, and defer post-quantum work"],"a":[1],"e":"Harvest now, decrypt later threatens key exchange today, so that goes first. Hybrid exchange protects capable clients while older clients keep working, which is crypto agility in practice. Certificates can wait because forging a signature needs a quantum computer at the time of the attack, not years later. Post-quantum-only would break older apps and partners. Bigger classical keys don't stop a quantum attack on key exchange."},
{"id":"n6","d":3,"q":"A company's single-tier Windows root CA is online, domain joined, and issues end-entity certificates directly, including about 6,000 EAP-TLS certificates used with Cisco ISE. Its private key sits in the software key store, and the server was in scope of a ransomware incident last year. The architect recommends a two-tier hierarchy with an HSM. Wireless outages during migration are not acceptable. Which migration approach is MOST appropriate?","o":["Take the current root offline now and have it sign a new HSM-backed issuing CA, so existing certificates stay valid and nothing needs re-enrollment or ISE changes","Renew the current root with a new key pair generated on an HSM, keeping the same subject name so relying parties continue to chain","Build a new offline HSM root and issuing CA, add the new root to endpoint and ISE trust stores, then re-enroll while the old CA keeps publishing CRLs","Revoke the current root and its certificates, then re-enroll all endpoints from a new two-tier hierarchy during a weekend change window"],"a":[2],"e":"The old root key may be compromised, so trust in it should not be carried forward. Building a new hierarchy and staging trust before re-enrollment avoids outages, while the old CA keeps revocation working until its certificates expire. Signing a subordinate with the old root extends a possibly compromised trust anchor. Renewing on the same server with the same name keeps the exposure and makes chain building confusing. Mass revocation in one weekend risks the wireless outage the business ruled out."},
{"id":"n7","d":3,"q":"An online retailer already fronts its web storefront with a CDN. Last week a 400 Gbps attack spread across every address in its public range, hitting the remote access VPN, mail gateway, and voice trunks, and saturating both 10 Gbps ISP links. The on-premises DDoS appliance sat behind the saturated links. The CFO wants protection that covers non-web services without re-architecting them this year. Which change BEST addresses this?","o":["Upgrade both ISP links to 100 Gbps and move the DDoS appliance to the provider edge so it filters before the customer links","Move the VPN, mail, and voice services behind the CDN's proxy features, with origin addresses restricted to the CDN","Contract an on-demand cloud scrubbing service that takes over the whole address range during attacks and returns clean traffic","Arrange upstream blackholing with both ISPs, so attacked addresses are dropped at the provider edge once traffic thresholds are crossed"],"a":[2],"e":"Volumetric attacks have to be absorbed upstream of the links they saturate. A scrubbing service that can take over the whole range covers every service without changing them. Bigger links are still far smaller than attack capacity. Putting VPN, mail, and voice behind a CDN is the re-architecture the CFO ruled out. Blackholing stops the attack by dropping the services too, which hands the attacker the outcome it wanted."},
{"id":"n8","d":3,"q":"Staff were redirected to a fake login page for a SaaS provider whose zone is DNSSEC signed. Internal Windows DNS servers forward all external queries to the ISP's resolvers and trust their answers. The company's own domain is already signed. Endpoints use the internal DNS servers, and the network team plans a migration to a new IPAM product next year. Which control MOST directly prevents a repeat?","o":["Enable DNSSEC validation on the internal recursive resolvers and stop relying on upstream answers without validating them","Deploy DNS over HTTPS from endpoints to the internal resolvers so queries cannot be modified on the local network","Confirm source port randomization and 0x20 case randomization are enabled on the internal resolvers to raise the cost of spoofing","Add NSEC3 to the company's own signed zone and shorten its RRSIG validity to limit replay of old records"],"a":[0],"e":"The target zone is signed, so a validating resolver would reject forged answers. Today the internal servers accept whatever the forwarders return. DoH protects the endpoint-to-resolver hop, not the resolver's cache. Port and case randomization make spoofing harder but offer no cryptographic check. Changes to the company's own zone protect others who resolve its names, not its staff resolving a third party. The IPAM migration is noise."},
{"id":"n9","d":3,"q":"A retailer has SPF and DKIM on its main domain and has run DMARC at p=none for two years. Customers keep receiving spoofed invoices. Marketing uses three third-party senders, finance uses a billing platform, and nobody has reviewed aggregate reports. The CMO worries that a policy change will block campaign email during the holiday season, which starts in six weeks. What should the architect do FIRST?","o":["Publish p=reject with pct=10 to start enforcement on a small share of mail and raise the percentage weekly","Analyze DMARC aggregate reports to inventory legitimate senders and get each aligned through DKIM or SPF","Add each vendor's sending IP ranges to the SPF record so that third-party mail passes authentication","Move marketing mail to a dedicated subdomain and publish p=reject on the main domain immediately"],"a":[1],"e":"Enforcement is only safe once every legitimate sender is known and aligned, and the aggregate reports are where that inventory comes from. Partial reject before that risks blocking real mail during the holidays. Adding vendor ranges to SPF hits the 10-lookup limit and may still fail alignment. A marketing subdomain is a good later step, but rejecting on the main domain before finding the billing platform and others can break invoices."},
{"id":"n10","d":3,"q":"A hospital is deploying 900 infusion pumps whose wireless supports only an older shared-passphrase mode. The vendor's FDA clearance prohibits firmware changes, and the default passphrase is printed in the service manual. The hospital has a NAC platform and a centrally managed wireless network. Nurses move pumps between floors constantly. Biomed asks for a design that limits the damage if one pump's credentials leak. Which design is BEST?","o":["One dedicated network with a strong shared passphrase that biomed rotates quarterly, isolated so pumps reach only the pump server","A mixed-mode wireless network where pumps use the older protocol and newer clients negotiate stronger encryption on the same network","A hidden network that admits only known hardware addresses, using the vendor passphrase, with access limited to the pump server","A unique credential per pump or small pump group, assigned centrally, with each device restricted to reaching only the pump server"],"a":[3],"e":"The goal is limiting the damage from one leaked credential. Unique per-device credentials mean a leak exposes one pump or group, which can be revoked without touching the rest, and the access restriction limits what a stolen credential can reach. A rotated shared passphrase still exposes every pump until the next rotation and makes each rotation a fleet-wide event. Mixed mode does nothing for the pumps' own weak credential. Hidden networks and address lists are easy to bypass and still rely on the published vendor passphrase."},
{"id":"n11","d":3,"q":"A data center hosts about 4,000 workloads across VMware, bare metal, and Kubernetes, with frequent application changes. Firewall rule requests take three weeks, and the CISO wants east-west segmentation in place this fiscal year. No one has a reliable map of application dependencies, and two prior segmentation attempts caused outages. The team has selected a host-based microsegmentation platform. What should the architect do FIRST?","o":["Write default-deny label-based policies for the crown-jewel applications and enforce them during the next maintenance window","Deploy agents in visibility mode, build dependency maps from observed flows, and validate them with application owners","Re-IP critical applications into dedicated VLANs so the existing firewalls can enforce zone-based policy between them","Define a labeling scheme by environment, application, and tier, and push it to the CMDB as the source of truth"],"a":[1],"e":"Prior outages came from enforcing without knowing real dependencies, so the first step is observing flows and confirming them with owners. Enforcing default deny first repeats the old failure. Re-IPping into VLANs is slow and brings back the change bottleneck. A labeling scheme is needed, but it should be informed by the observed data and is not the first step on its own."},
{"id":"n13","d":3,"q":"A misconfigured bucket exposed customer files last quarter. The cloud team proposes an organization-wide block on public object storage access. Marketing objects, saying its product images and press kits are served directly from public buckets and that the website must not change before the spring launch. The company runs about 300 accounts under one organization. Which approach BEST prevents a recurrence while meeting the business need?","o":["Enforce the block org-wide, and serve marketing content from a dedicated account via a CDN using origin access control on private buckets","Enable CSPM auto-remediation that removes public access from buckets within 15 minutes unless they carry an approved, time-limited exception tag","Require a security review ticket before a bucket is made public, with a quarterly attestation from each account owner","Enable data discovery scanning on all buckets and alert when a public bucket contains personal or customer data"],"a":[0],"e":"A preventive org-level guardrail stops the misconfiguration at its source, and a CDN with origin access control lets marketing publish content without public buckets. Auto-remediation leaves an exposure window and tag exceptions can be abused. Ticket and attestation processes depend on people following them, which already failed. Data discovery detects exposure after the fact."},
{"id":"n14","d":3,"q":"A development team runs a customer portal on a managed application platform (PaaS) that runs their container images. A penetration test returns four findings. The team lead asks the architect which finding the team is LEAST likely to be responsible for fixing, so they can open a ticket with the provider.","o":["A vulnerable logging library bundled inside the team's container image","TLS 1.0 still accepted, where the platform exposes a minimum TLS version setting","An outdated kernel on the shared hosts that run the platform's containers","A managed identity for the app holding contributor rights on the subscription"],"a":[2],"e":"In PaaS the provider manages the host operating system and kernel, so that finding belongs with the provider. The team owns what it packages in its image, the configuration options the platform exposes to it, such as minimum TLS version, and the permissions it grants to the app's identity."},
{"id":"n15","d":3,"q":"A shared Kubernetes platform enforces strict pod security in application namespaces. A vendor's monitoring agent runs privileged in its own namespace under an approved exception. An application team now asks to give its CI jobs direct access to the node's container runtime so they can build images inside the cluster, and points to the vendor exception as precedent. Their release is in two weeks. What should the architect recommend?","o":["Grant a scoped exception like the vendor's for the CI namespace only, and add runtime detection for container escape attempts","Allow the access but run the CI jobs in a sandboxed runtime so their system calls are isolated from the host kernel","Move the CI namespace to a looser security profile that permits more host access, and require signed images there","Deny the request and move image builds to a builder that doesn't need host access, ideally on a separate build cluster"],"a":[3],"e":"Direct access to the container runtime is effectively root on the node, which defeats the isolation the platform depends on, and builders exist that don't need it. An exception for a vetted vendor agent isn't precedent for CI jobs that run untrusted code. Sandboxing system calls doesn't limit what the runtime can be told to do. A looser profile weakens the boundary, and signing images doesn't address host access."},
{"id":"n16","d":3,"q":"A company has EDR on 50,000 laptops. In a red team exercise, testers used a phished help desk account with PowerShell and WMI to move laterally for three weeks without an alert. Afterward, the SOC found the relevant EDR telemetry had already aged out after seven days. The help desk relies on PowerShell daily. The SOC has six analysts. Which change MOST improves detection of this kind of activity?","o":["Enforce WDAC application control with PowerShell in Constrained Language Mode for all users outside the IT administrators group","Replace the EDR product with one whose vendor publishes higher detection scores in independent evaluations","Add a network sandbox at the internet edge to detonate attachments and downloads before they reach users","Retain EDR and script block logs centrally for 90+ days and build behavioral detections for admin tool use by unusual accounts"],"a":[3],"e":"The attack used legitimate tools and the evidence aged out, so longer central retention plus behavior-based detections addresses both gaps. Application control is prevention, and help desk users need full PowerShell, so it would not cover this account. Swapping vendors does not fix the retention or detection content problem. A sandbox looks at files entering the network, while this activity used built-in tools."},
{"id":"n17","d":3,"q":"Server administrators reach critical systems through a PAM bastion with MFA and just-in-time elevation. They connect from their everyday laptops, which they also use for email and web browsing. In a recent red team test, a phished admin's laptop was implanted, and the testers rode the admin's authenticated bastion session after MFA. Budget allows one major change this year. Which change BEST addresses the finding?","o":["Switch bastion MFA to number matching push so admins cannot approve prompts they did not start","Issue dedicated privileged access workstations, and configure the bastion to accept sessions only from those devices","Cut the idle timeout on bastion sessions to five minutes and require re-authentication before elevated commands are allowed to run","Record all bastion sessions and stream keystroke logs to the SIEM with alerts on high-risk commands"],"a":[1],"e":"The weakness is the compromised source device, which can hijack any session it starts. Dedicated, locked-down workstations that the bastion requires remove that path. Number matching stops MFA fatigue, which was not the attack. Shorter timeouts and re-authentication still run from the implanted laptop. Recording helps detect and investigate but does not prevent the hijack."},
{"id":"n18","d":3,"q":"A call center application stores card numbers so agents can look up customers when they read a full card number over the phone. The company wants to pull the call center app and its database out of PCI DSS scope before next year's assessment. The database team has offered transparent data encryption, which is already licensed. Which design BEST meets both needs?","o":["Use format-preserving encryption in the application with keys in a KMS, so card fields keep their format and can be searched by encrypting the input","Replace card numbers with tokens from a separate vault, and look up records by token or by a keyed hash computed inside the vault","Enable transparent data encryption on the database with keys in an HSM, and limit query access to the call center service account","Store only the first six and last four digits and match callers by combining those digits with name and postal code"],"a":[1],"e":"Tokens and a keyed hash computed in the vault let agents find records while the app and database never hold card numbers or keys, which supports removing them from scope. Format-preserving encryption in the app keeps keys and card data in the app, so it stays in scope. TDE protects files at rest but the database still serves card numbers. Truncated digits plus name is a weaker match and does not support lookup by full number."},
{"id":"n19","d":3,"q":"A bank wants to run fraud analytics on customer transaction data in a public cloud. The risk committee's specific concern is a malicious cloud operator or compromised hypervisor reading data while jobs run. The analytics use standard Spark jobs that must finish within a nightly four-hour window. Keys are already held in the bank's own HSM. Which design BEST addresses the committee's concern?","o":["Use customer-managed keys from the bank's HSM for disk and object encryption, with key use logged and restricted to the analytics role","Run jobs in confidential VMs with memory encryption, and release data keys from the bank's HSM only after remote attestation succeeds","Run the analytics on homomorphically encrypted data so the cloud provider only processes ciphertext, not plaintext values","Place the jobs on dedicated hosts so no other tenant's workloads share the hardware with the bank's analytics cluster"],"a":[1],"e":"The threat is the operator or hypervisor reading data in memory. Confidential VMs encrypt memory, and attestation-gated key release means keys only go to a verified environment. Customer-managed keys protect data at rest, but data is in clear in memory during processing. Homomorphic encryption fits the threat but is far too slow for general Spark analytics in a four-hour window. Dedicated hosts remove other tenants, not the provider's own hypervisor."},
{"id":"n20","d":3,"q":"A retailer is rolling out SD-WAN to 300 stores with direct internet access at each site. Stores have no local IT, the network team is three people, and leadership wants to retire the aging branch firewalls rather than refresh them. Each store also has a PCI-scoped POS VLAN that must reach only the payment processor. The CISO wants one web and SaaS security policy everywhere. Which design BEST fits?","o":["Replace branch firewalls with NGFWs at every store, managed from a central console with shared policy templates and zero-touch provisioning","Backhaul all store internet traffic over the SD-WAN to the data center firewalls for central inspection and logging","Allow direct breakout only to an allowlist of sanctioned SaaS domains, with DNS filtering on the SD-WAN edge device","Steer store internet traffic from SD-WAN edges to a cloud SSE for central policy, and keep POS flows segmented to the processor"],"a":[3],"e":"A cloud SSE gives one policy for all sites without branch hardware, which fits a small team retiring firewalls, and SD-WAN segmentation keeps the POS VLAN scoped. NGFWs at 300 stores would work technically but means a hardware refresh the business wants to avoid. Backhauling defeats direct internet access. A SaaS allowlist with DNS filtering does not give full web inspection and consistent policy."},
{"id":"n21","d":3,"q":"A regional bank's core system must meet an RTO of 2 hours and an RPO of 5 minutes, set by its regulator. The recovery site is 400 km away, and the CFO has said the budget for this project is flat. The application is sensitive to write latency. The bank has never done a full failover test, and the last DR plan update was three years ago. Which design BEST meets the objectives?","o":["Synchronous storage replication to the recovery site so committed writes exist at both sites before acknowledgment","Asynchronous replication on short cycles, with an automated failover runbook that is tested on a regular schedule","Synchronous replication to a nearby bunker site, cascading asynchronously to the 400 km site for regional events","Database log shipping every 15 minutes to the recovery site, with daily automated restore tests of the shipped logs"],"a":[1],"e":"Asynchronous replication with short cycles meets a 5-minute RPO without adding write latency, and regular tested failover addresses the untested 2-hour RTO. Synchronous replication over 400 km adds round-trip delay to every write. A three-site cascade exceeds the requirement and the flat budget. Log shipping every 15 minutes misses the 5-minute RPO."},
{"id":"n22","d":3,"q":"Backups go to an object store with object lock in governance mode and 30-day retention. The backup servers are domain joined, and backup admins sign in with their AD accounts. In a peer company's incident, attackers with domain admin used the backup console to shorten retention and delete restore points before encrypting production. Nightly jobs complete on time and the storage team has spare capacity. Which change BEST addresses this attack path?","o":["Switch object lock to compliance mode and move backup administration into a separate identity domain with phishing-resistant MFA","Install EDR on the backup servers and alert on any change to retention policies made through the backup console","Increase backup frequency to every four hours so that less data is lost if production is encrypted after an attack","Add a monthly tape copy stored offsite with a courier service, kept for a full year as an air-gapped last resort if online copies are lost"],"a":[0],"e":"Governance mode lets sufficiently privileged users shorten or bypass retention, and domain admin can reach the backup console. Compliance mode prevents shortening retention, and a separate identity domain stops a domain compromise from reaching backup admin. EDR alerts are detective and may come too late. More frequent backups do not protect them from deletion. Monthly tape helps as a last resort but gives a poor recovery point."},
{"id":"n23","d":3,"q":"A city is deploying 20,000 environmental sensors on public lampposts. They connect over cellular to a cloud IoT hub. The threat model assumes attackers will take some devices to a lab and, with enough effort, extract any secret stored on them. The vendor proposes a lower-cost module without a secure element. Which design decision MOST limits the impact of one captured device?","o":["Encrypt firmware with a fleet-wide key so that captured devices cannot be reverse engineered by attackers","Unique per-device keys and certificates kept in a secure element, with the hub able to revoke a single device","Blow the JTAG and debug fuses during manufacturing so the hardware debug interfaces are disabled in the field","Use mutual TLS to the hub with a fleet client certificate and pin the hub's certificate in the device firmware"],"a":[1],"e":"If secrets can eventually be extracted, the goal is that one device's secrets are useless for others. Unique identities let the hub revoke one device, and a secure element raises extraction cost. A fleet-wide firmware key or shared client certificate means one extraction compromises all devices. Disabling debug fuses raises the bar but does not limit impact once a key is extracted."},
{"id":"n24","d":3,"q":"A partner-facing order application supports only an obsolete TLS version, and the vendor's replacement is a year out. Partners connect from the internet, and an auditor flagged the protocol. The application server also runs a nightly batch job that loads inventory files from an internal share. Which approach is BEST for the coming year?","o":["Put a reverse proxy in front that offers current TLS to partners, and keep the old protocol only on an isolated internal leg","Keep the old protocol but limit it to its strongest available cipher options, and record the auditor's finding as addressed","Document a twelve-month risk acceptance signed by the business owner, with a compensating IPS signature at the internet edge","Require partners to connect over site-to-site VPN tunnels so the weak session runs only inside an encrypted tunnel"],"a":[0],"e":"The proxy fixes what partners and the auditor see, keeps the weak protocol off the internet, and needs nothing from the vendor or partners. Tuning cipher options doesn't fix a protocol that is itself deprecated. Risk acceptance alone leaves the exposure in place for a year when a cheap control exists. Forcing VPNs on every partner is a heavy lift for them and leaves the weak protocol running end to end. The batch job is noise."},
{"id":"n25","d":3,"q":"An architect is designing password storage for a new citizen portal that will run under a federal contract requiring FIPS 140 validated cryptography for all security functions. The identity team has benchmarked several options on the target hardware and wants strong resistance to GPU cracking. The portal expects about two million accounts. Which approach is BEST?","o":["Argon2id with memory and time costs tuned for about 250 ms per hash and a unique salt for each password","bcrypt with a cost factor of 12 and a unique salt per password, upgrading the cost as hardware gets faster","PBKDF2 with HMAC-SHA-256, a high iteration count, a unique salt, and an optional pepper held in an HSM","SHA-512 with a unique salt and a secret pepper stored in the HSM, applied once per password at login"],"a":[2],"e":"The FIPS requirement is the deciding fact. PBKDF2 is an approved key derivation function, and a high iteration count plus an HSM-held pepper adds GPU resistance. Argon2id and bcrypt are stronger against GPUs but are not FIPS-approved algorithms. A single salted SHA-512 is too fast, even with a pepper."},
{"id":"n26","d":3,"q":"A company is opening an engineering office in a country that requires import licensing for encryption products and allows authorities to demand decryption keys. Engineers there will access source code and customer data hosted in the EU. The network team has already shipped standard IPsec routers to the site. Which approach is BEST?","o":["Use the country's national algorithm suite for the local links so the deployment fits the local encryption rules","Get legal advice on licensing and key disclosure there, and limit the data reachable from that office based on the findings","Turn up standard IPsec with AES-256 as shipped, since commercial VPN products usually fall under mass-market exemptions","Use a carrier private MPLS circuit without encryption to that office, so no encryption product needs a local import license or key disclosure"],"a":[1],"e":"The legal requirements decide what can be deployed and what data can be safely exposed, so counsel and data-scoping come first. National algorithms may or may not be required and do not address key disclosure. Assuming an exemption risks breaking local law. Dropping encryption exposes source code and customer data on the carrier network."},
{"id":"n27","d":3,"q":"A colocation suite's loading dock opens into a corridor that leads to the server room, the meet-me room, and two network closets. The server room has a badge reader and a camera, but the closets use keyed locks. Delivery drivers often walk through unescorted while staff are busy. Facilities has budget for one project this year. Which control BEST addresses the risk?","o":["Formalize an escort policy for drivers with a sign-in log at the dock that is reviewed by security every week","Add badge readers to the closets and the meet-me room, with alerts on doors held open for more than 30 seconds","Install CCTV with analytics in the corridor that flags unbadged people walking past the dock area entrance","Build a delivery staging area at the dock with its own access-controlled door into the secure corridor"],"a":[3],"e":"Separating the dock from the secure corridor removes the uncontrolled path for every room it serves. Escort rules already fail when staff are busy. Readers on individual doors help but leave drivers in the corridor next to sensitive rooms. CCTV analytics detect rather than prevent."},
{"id":"n28","d":3,"q":"A company's SWG already decrypts most web traffic. Microsoft 365 is the only sanctioned collaboration suite, but the CISO suspects dozens of unsanctioned SaaS tools are in use, some holding customer data. The CIO wants a list of what to block before the next board meeting, and legal wants an acceptable use update. A CASB has just been purchased. What should the architect do FIRST?","o":["Configure the SWG to block all SaaS categories except Microsoft 365 until each app goes through a review process","Connect the CASB to the Microsoft 365 tenant through its API to apply DLP to sharing and stored content right away","Feed SWG and firewall logs into the CASB's discovery function to identify apps in use, their users, and their risk","Publish an updated acceptable use policy that bans unsanctioned SaaS and requires staff to report the tools they use"],"a":[2],"e":"Discovery from existing logs gives the factual inventory that blocking, policy, and board reporting depend on. Blocking everything first will break business processes no one knows about. API DLP for the sanctioned tenant is valuable but does not address unknown apps. A policy update matters but is uninformed without discovery."},
{"id":"n29","d":3,"q":"A retailer with 40 stores and a flat corporate network faces its first PCI DSS assessment in nine months. Stores connect over SD-WAN. The QSA has said the whole network will be in scope unless segmentation is proven. The CFO wants scope reduced to save audit cost, and the network team already uses VLANs widely. Which approach BEST meets these goals?","o":["Treat the whole network as in scope and apply PCI hardening, logging, and scanning across all systems","Place CDE systems on their own VLANs with switch ACLs that block traffic from user and guest VLANs","Isolate the CDE behind default-deny filtering with documented flows, and prove it with segmentation testing","Encrypt all internal traffic with IPsec so that systems outside the CDE cannot read cardholder data in transit"],"a":[2],"e":"Scope reduction needs both a default-deny boundary with documented flows and evidence from segmentation testing that the boundary works. Treating everything as in scope is valid but defeats the cost goal. VLANs with ACLs blocking only some sources are not default deny and lack validation. Encrypting traffic does not remove connected systems from scope."},
{"id":"n30","d":3,"q":"A private cloud runs PCI workloads and developer test VMs on the same vSphere clusters. Hypervisor patches are applied quarterly because of change freezes. A new advisory describes a guest-to-host escape bug with a public exploit, and the developer VMs run code pulled from public repositories. Which design decision MOST reduces the impact of a future escape?","o":["Enable vTPM and secure boot on all guests so that tampered guest operating systems are detected at startup","Separate workloads of different trust levels onto distinct clusters so a host escape cannot reach PCI guests","Apply distributed firewall rules between developer and PCI VMs so that they cannot talk to each other over the network","Encrypt PCI VM disks with keys from an external KMS so a compromised host cannot read the stored data"],"a":[1],"e":"An escape gives access to the host and everything it runs, so separate clusters for different trust levels contain the impact. Guest secure boot protects the guest, not the hypervisor. Network rules do not stop a host-level attacker. VM encryption keys are handled by the host, so a compromised host can read the data."},
{"id":"n31","d":3,"q":"An architect is adding an inline IPS in front of a payment API. The business fears two things: false positives blocking customer transactions, and an outage if the IPS itself fails. The vendor's default policy has about 30,000 signatures. Peak traffic is during a holiday promotion that starts in four weeks. Which deployment approach is BEST?","o":["Deploy inline in blocking mode with the vendor's recommended policy, and add exceptions as soon as customers report failures","Deploy inline with fail-open bypass in detect mode, tune against real traffic, then block on high-confidence signatures","Deploy out of band on a SPAN port and send TCP resets for matched attacks, so the inline path carries no new device","Deploy inline in blocking mode and fail closed, so that payment traffic cannot pass without being inspected"],"a":[1],"e":"Fail-open bypass protects availability, and detect mode with tuning protects against false positives before blocking starts. Blocking first with fixes after complaints harms customers during the promotion. Out-of-band resets are racy and often miss. Fail closed turns any IPS failure into a payment outage."},
{"id":"n32","d":3,"q":"A company is rolling out wired port authentication for corporate laptops using machine certificates from its PKI, with laptops verifying the authentication server. Printers and phones will be admitted by hardware address on separate ports. A pen tester notes that conference rooms have exposed wall jacks used by laptops. Which remaining risk for the laptop ports is MOST significant, and what addresses it?","o":["Stolen user passwords used to authenticate, addressed by adding a second factor to the laptop network sign-in","A rogue authentication server capturing credentials, addressed by moving the laptops to a password-based authentication method","A device placed between an authenticated laptop and the jack to ride its session, addressed with link-layer encryption","Spoofing a printer's hardware address to get access, addressed by profiling devices before admitting them by address"],"a":[2],"e":"Port authentication checks the device once, at connect time. After that, a device inserted inline can relay the laptop's traffic and inject its own, because the switch only sees an authorized port. Encrypting and authenticating every frame between laptop and switch stops that. Certificates don't use passwords, so MFA on passwords doesn't apply, and moving to passwords makes things worse. Printer spoofing is real, but it's a risk on the printer ports, not the laptop ports the question asks about."},
{"id":"n33","d":3,"q":"A research institute shares a Fibre Channel SAN across several departments. The threat model includes an attacker with root on one department's server who can change that server's HBA WWPN. Zoning today is soft zoning by WWPN. The storage team is also evaluating a NAS refresh next year. Which control MOST directly prevents that server from reaching other departments' LUNs?","o":["Enable CHAP authentication on the storage arrays and require each host to authenticate when it logs in","Use port-based hard zoning on the fabric switches together with LUN masking tied to each host's ports","Encrypt each department's LUNs at the host with keys from a central KMS so other hosts read only ciphertext","Keep WWPN zoning but alert in the SAN management tool when a WWPN logs in from an unexpected port"],"a":[1],"e":"A root user who can spoof a WWPN defeats WWPN-based zoning, so enforcement tied to physical switch ports plus LUN masking is the direct control. CHAP applies to iSCSI, not Fibre Channel logins. Host encryption protects confidentiality but still lets the server reach and damage the LUNs. Alerting is detective. The NAS refresh is noise."},
{"id":"n34","d":3,"q":"A regulator requires that a payment company's keys reside in FIPS 140-3 Level 3 HSMs under single-tenant control. The company also wants native cloud services such as object storage and managed databases to encrypt with those keys without custom code. The team has two engineers with HSM experience. Which option BEST fits?","o":["Use the provider's standard shared key service, since its underlying hardware already carries the required validation level","Generate keys on premises and import them into the provider's standard shared key service through its import process","Deploy dedicated single-tenant cloud HSMs and have each application call them directly through custom integration code","Connect the provider's key service to a dedicated single-tenant cloud HSM, so native services encrypt with keys held there"],"a":[3],"e":"Only this option meets both needs: keys live in single-tenant HSMs under the company's control, and native services keep working through the key service integration. The standard service is multi-tenant, which fails the regulator's control requirement however the hardware is validated. Importing keys changes who generated them, not where they live. Calling HSMs directly meets the control requirement but breaks native service encryption and puts custom code on a two-person team."},
{"id":"n35","d":3,"q":"A company wants employees to read corporate email and documents on personal iOS and Android phones. A works council has ruled that personal devices may not be enrolled in any device management. The company uses Microsoft 365 and Intune, and legal wants corporate data removable when someone leaves. Which approach BEST balances security and privacy?","o":["App protection policies on approved apps without enrollment, enforced by conditional access, with selective wipe","An Android Enterprise work profile and iOS user enrollment, so corporate data sits in a separate, company-managed container","Access through a virtual mobile infrastructure app that streams a remote Android session to each phone","Browser-only access to Outlook on the web and SharePoint with downloads blocked by session controls"],"a":[0],"e":"App protection without enrollment protects corporate data inside managed apps, conditional access makes those apps mandatory, and selective wipe removes only corporate data. Work profiles and user enrollment are privacy-friendly but still require enrollment, which the works council banned. Virtual mobile infrastructure works but is costly and awkward for daily email. Browser-only access is safer but degrades usability more than needed."},
{"id":"n36","d":3,"q":"A branch design for 60 small offices uses an ISP-supplied router doing PAT, with no separate firewall. No port forwards are configured. The vendor argues NAT already acts as a firewall. During review, the architect notices that the ISP enables IPv6 by default and delegates a /56 to each site. Which concern is MOST significant?","o":["PAT tables can be exhausted by a flood of outbound connections, causing a denial of service for users across the branch","NAT interferes with IPsec AH, so future site-to-site tunnels to headquarters will fail to establish","Hosts get global IPv6 addresses that PAT does not touch, so inbound access depends on explicit IPv6 filtering","Hiding internal IPv4 addresses relies on obscurity, which auditors may reject as a documented control"],"a":[2],"e":"With IPv6 delegated, hosts are directly addressable and NAT plays no part, so unless the router filters inbound IPv6, they are exposed. PAT exhaustion is a real but minor availability issue. AH incompatibility is true but most site-to-site designs use ESP with NAT traversal. The obscurity point is weaker than a concrete exposure."},
{"id":"n37","d":3,"q":"A data center edge uses an active/passive pair of next-generation firewalls managed from a central console. During a planned failover test, the passive unit took over with an older threat signature set and without a rule change an engineer had made directly on the active unit the week before. Sessions survived the failover and users noticed nothing. Which design consideration is MOST important to fix?","o":["Synchronize sessions between peers so established connections survive failover without interrupting users","Keep configuration and content versions identical on both peers, with changes made only through central management","Tune link and path monitoring with preemption so the preferred unit reclaims the active role once it has recovered","Move the pair to active/active so both units carry production traffic and each one stays warm with current state"],"a":[1],"e":"The security gap is that the standby enforced older signatures and policy. Config and content sync, with changes only through central management, make both peers enforce the same rules. Session sync is already working and is about availability. Monitoring and preemption tune failover behavior, not policy parity. Active/active adds complexity and does not prevent out-of-band local changes."},
{"id":"n38","d":3,"q":"A remote mine site has a satellite link that drops for hours at a time. Shift workers share about 40 workstations and must sign in during outages. The site's server cabinet sits in a dusty room with a basic lock, and theft of equipment has happened before. Headquarters runs Active Directory and wants to keep sign-in centralized. Which design is BEST?","o":["A writable domain controller at the site with BitLocker so all accounts can sign in during link outages","A read-only domain controller with a password replication policy that caches only that site's users","Rely on cached logon credentials on each workstation, raising the cache count to cover the shift workers","Move sign-in to a cloud identity provider with passwordless authentication and offline token caching"],"a":[1],"e":"An RODC lets site users sign in during outages while limiting what a stolen server exposes to that site's cached accounts, and it cannot write changes back to the domain. A writable DC exposes the whole directory if stolen, even with disk encryption it is a bigger target. Cached logons only work for users who already signed in on that workstation, which fails on shared machines. A cloud IdP needs connectivity for most sign-ins."},
{"id":"n40","d":3,"q":"An aerospace firm shares engineering drawings with about 50 suppliers, each with its own Microsoft 365 tenant. Supplier machinists open the files offline on shop floor CAD workstations. Export control rules require the firm to be able to revoke access when a contract ends. The firm also plans to move file shares to SharePoint next year. Which approach BEST meets the requirements?","o":["Apply DLP that blocks emailing drawings outside the company and route all supplier sharing through internal staff","Send drawings as password-protected PDFs, with each supplier's password sent separately through a different channel such as SMS","Apply encrypting sensitivity labels granting rights to each supplier's identities, with expiry and revocable access","Share drawings through a secure partner portal with view-only access and dynamic watermarks on each page shown"],"a":[2],"e":"Encrypting labels travel with the file, work offline once a license is cached, grant rights to external identities, and support expiry and revocation. DLP blocking stops the sharing the business needs. Password-protected files cannot be revoked once the password is known. A view-only portal fails the offline workstation requirement. The SharePoint move is noise."},
{"id":"n41","d":3,"q":"A new data center fabric uses an SDN controller cluster that programs forwarding and microsegmentation policy on 200 leaf switches. The vendor's reference design puts the controller's northbound API on the general management VLAN, reachable by IT staff workstations with local accounts. Uptime is important, but the CISO's question is about compromise. Which control for the controller is MOST important?","o":["Isolate controller interfaces on a dedicated management network, with mutual TLS to switches and MFA and RBAC on its API","Run the controller as a three-node cluster across separate failure domains so losing one node does not affect policy programming","Encrypt the VXLAN overlay between leaf switches so tenant traffic cannot be read if someone taps the fabric links","Configure leaf switches to keep forwarding with their last known policy if they lose contact with the controller"],"a":[0],"e":"Whoever controls the controller controls policy for the whole fabric, so locking down access to it and authenticating its communication with switches matters most. Clustering and headless forwarding are availability measures. Encrypting overlay traffic protects the data plane, not the control point."},
{"id":"n42","d":3,"q":"A company is centralizing logs from 3,000 devices across 60 sites into a SIEM over links it does not trust. About a third of the devices are older switches and controllers that can only send logs unencrypted. Some logs include usernames and internal addresses, and links drop several times a month. The SIEM vendor licenses by daily volume. Which design is MOST appropriate?","o":["Send all logs over the existing encrypted SD-WAN overlay as they are, since the tunnels already protect traffic between sites","Require encrypted log transport on every device, and replace hardware that can't support it at the next refresh cycle","Collect logs locally on a protected segment at each site, then forward them encrypted and buffered to survive outages","Have the SIEM pull data from each device over an encrypted management protocol so traffic is protected on the wire"],"a":[2],"e":"Local collection meets old devices where they are, keeps their unencrypted traffic off untrusted links, and buffering covers the drops. It also gives a place to filter before volume-based licensing. The overlay leaves gaps wherever traffic leaves it and does nothing for outages. Requiring encryption everywhere leaves a third of devices unprotected until a refresh. Polling misses events between polls and isn't how most devices report logs."},
{"id":"n43","d":3,"q":"A logistics firm is moving its warehouse system to IaaS. The application team wants SSH open from 0.0.0.0/0 to every VM so contractors can troubleshoot from wherever they are traveling. Contractors use their own laptops, and the security team wants MFA and a record of what was done. The cloud provider offers a native session management service. Which approach is BEST?","o":["Allow SSH only from each contractor's home office IP range, and update the security group when they travel","Use the provider's session manager or an identity-aware proxy with MFA and session logs, keeping port 22 closed","Keep SSH open but require key-only authentication, disable root login, and run fail2ban on each of the VMs","Give contractors a client VPN into the VPC, then allow SSH only from the VPN address pool with per-user keys on each VM"],"a":[1],"e":"A session manager or identity-aware proxy needs no inbound port, enforces MFA through the identity provider, and records sessions. IP allowlists break for travelers and need constant edits. Hardened SSH open to the internet still exposes every VM to attack and does not add MFA or recording. A client VPN puts unmanaged laptops on the network and still lacks session recording."},
{"id":"n44","d":3,"q":"A legacy system stores two billion records encrypted under one key in a mode where identical records produce identical ciphertext. The rewrite must protect confidentiality and integrity, and support lookups by account number. The team wants to keep the single key for simplicity. The rewrite has a 9-month deadline. Which design is BEST?","o":["Randomized encryption under the existing key for confidentiality, with the account number kept in a separate plaintext column","Deterministic encryption for every field so lookups still match, accepting that identical records still look identical","Authenticated encryption under the single existing key, re-encrypting all two billion records in one migration window","Authenticated encryption with keys rotated well within safe usage limits, plus a keyed hash index for account lookups"],"a":[3],"e":"The design needs integrity, no visible patterns, and searchability. Authenticated encryption gives confidentiality and integrity, rotating keys keeps each one within safe usage limits at this volume, and a keyed hash lets the app search by account number without exposing it. Randomized encryption without integrity misses a stated requirement, and a plaintext account number leaks data. Deterministic encryption on every field keeps the pattern leak. One key across billions of records pushes past safe limits for common authenticated modes."},
{"id":"n45","d":3,"q":"Fifty autoscaling replicas of a payment service share one AES-GCM key from a secrets manager and together encrypt about ten billion messages a day. A developer wants to use a static nonce because the key is rotated daily. A second proposal uses a counter starting at zero on each replica, saved across restarts. Which nonce design is SAFEST?","o":["Random 96-bit nonces for each message under the shared daily key, generated with a CSPRNG on each replica","A per-replica counter starting at zero and saved across restarts, used under the shared daily key","Per-replica subkeys derived with HKDF from the shared key and replica ID, each with its own message counter","A static nonce with the key rotated every hour instead of every day, to shorten the window in which reuse matters"],"a":[2],"e":"Per-replica subkeys with counters give each replica its own key space, so nonces never repeat under one key. Random 96-bit nonces at ten billion messages a day exceed the roughly 2^32 per-key limit, so collisions become likely. Counters starting at zero on every replica collide across replicas under the shared key. Any static nonce reuse lets attackers recover plaintext relationships and forge tags."},
{"id":"n46","d":3,"q":"A manufacturer runs workloads on premises, in two IaaS providers, and on edge servers in 30 plants. Each environment has its own team and tools. The CISO wants consistent workload protection, and the board approved budget for one cross-environment platform next year. An auditor recently found plant servers missing from every inventory. What should the architect do FIRST?","o":["Select a CWPP that supports all three environments and begin agent rollout in the largest public cloud","Standardize each environment on one cloud provider's native security tools to cut the number of consoles","Build a unified workload inventory and define a protection baseline by workload type for all environments","Require each team to report monthly compliance against its own tool's benchmark to a central executive dashboard"],"a":[2],"e":"Consistency needs a single view of what exists and a common definition of required protection, and the auditor showed the inventory is incomplete. Tool selection should follow those requirements. One provider's native tools may not cover the plants or other clouds. Monthly reports against different benchmarks keep the inconsistency in place."},
{"id":"n47","d":3,"q":"A hospital network contains thousands of unmanaged medical and IoT devices, many on old operating systems that cannot run agents. Some are life-support adjacent, and biomed reports that an aggressive scan once rebooted an imaging modality. The CISO wants these devices segmented within a year. What should the architect recommend FIRST?","o":["Run credentialed active scans after hours to build an accurate inventory of each device and its OS version","Enable 802.1X on all switch ports so that unknown devices are placed in a quarantine VLAN on connection","Move all non-PC devices into a single IoT VLAN with a firewall between it and the clinical network","Use passive discovery from SPAN and flow data to identify devices and their communication patterns"],"a":[3],"e":"Passive discovery builds the inventory and flow map that segmentation needs, without touching fragile devices. Active scans have already caused a reboot. 802.1X will quarantine devices that cannot authenticate, which could disrupt care. A single IoT VLAN before knowing flows can break clinical workflows and still leaves devices in one large group."},
{"id":"n48","d":3,"q":"A company in the EU wants to inspect encrypted web traffic for malware and data loss. The works council and privacy counsel object to decrypting personal banking and health sites. Several business apps use certificate pinning, and one trading platform breaks when inspected. The SWG supports category-based policies. What is the BEST design?","o":["Decrypt all traffic for complete visibility, and limit access to decrypted content logs to two named security analysts","Decrypt by policy with bypasses for health, finance, and pinned apps, agreed with the works council and disclosed","Skip decryption and rely on endpoint EDR plus DNS filtering to detect malware and data loss on the laptops","Decrypt only for users flagged as high risk by the insider threat program, leaving everyone else uninspected"],"a":[1],"e":"Policy-based decryption with category and app bypasses addresses privacy and compatibility, and agreement with the works council plus disclosure is often legally required in the EU. Decrypting everything still processes personal data the council objects to and breaks pinned apps. No decryption leaves a large gap in web DLP. Targeting individuals raises its own legal problems and misses most risk."},
{"id":"n49","d":3,"q":"A travel company's public API serves its mobile app and about 20 partner integrations. Attackers are scraping fares through thousands of residential proxy IPs, each under the per-IP rate limit. IP reputation feeds show the addresses as clean consumer connections. Partners have API keys, and the mobile app has no attestation. Which control is MOST effective?","o":["Block traffic from countries where the company does not sell, and add IP reputation scoring at the gateway","Require a CAPTCHA on API calls coming from the mobile app or from unknown clients at the gateway","Enforce per-account and per-key quotas, plus bot management using app attestation and behavioral signals","Require mutual TLS for all API clients, embedding a client certificate in the mobile app at build time"],"a":[2],"e":"Distributed scraping defeats per-IP limits and reputation, so limits keyed to accounts and API keys, plus attestation and behavior signals that tell real apps from scripts, target the actual abuse. Residential proxies appear in every country and look clean. CAPTCHAs do not fit API calls. A certificate embedded in a mobile app can be extracted and reused."},
{"id":"n50","d":3,"q":"A company's cloud identity tenant has 14 permanent global administrators who use SMS-based MFA. Two of them also hold admin rights in the production subscription. A recent phishing kit targeted the company with real-time MFA relay. The SOC already alerts on role assignment changes. Which control MOST directly reduces the risk of a full environment takeover?","o":["Add a conditional access policy that limits admin sign-ins to corporate office IP ranges and named locations","Require phishing-resistant MFA for privileged roles and replace standing access with JIT elevation plus break-glass","Forward all audit logs to the SIEM with new detections for unusual admin sign-ins and for changes to tenant-wide policy","Convert the administrator accounts to cloud-only identities so on-premises AD compromise cannot reach them"],"a":[1],"e":"Real-time relay defeats SMS codes, and FIDO2 or certificate-based MFA blocks it, while JIT cuts the number of standing admin accounts an attacker can use. Location policies can be bypassed through proxies or compromised internal hosts. More detections are useful but act after the fact. Cloud-only accounts help with hybrid attacks but still use SMS MFA."},
{"id":"n51","d":3,"q":"A security architect is designing file integrity monitoring for 200 critical Linux servers. In a past incident, an attacker with root updated the local FIM baseline after modifying system binaries, so no alerts fired. The current tool hashes with SHA-1 and scans nightly. Which design change MOST improves the integrity of the monitoring itself?","o":["Switch the hashing algorithm from SHA-1 to SHA-256 to resist collision attacks against the stored baselines","Run scans every hour instead of nightly so changes are found sooner, before attackers can update the baseline","Keep signed baselines and policy off the host, compare centrally, and alert when an agent stops reporting","Store the local baseline database on a read-only partition that is remounted read-write only during patching"],"a":[2],"e":"The attacker edited the local baseline, so moving baselines and comparison off the host, signed, and watching for silent agents keeps a root user from hiding changes. SHA-256 is better but collisions were not the problem. More frequent scans still compare against a baseline the attacker can change. A root user can remount a read-only partition."},
{"id":"n52","d":3,"q":"After a ransomware incident that spread from one infected laptop to about 400 others over SMB and WinRM in under an hour, leadership asks for the network design change with the GREATEST effect on lateral movement. LAPS is already deployed, and admins use dedicated jump hosts for server work. Which network design change has the GREATEST impact?","o":["Use host firewall policy to block inbound SMB, RDP, and WinRM between workstations, except from the jump hosts","Place each floor's workstations on a separate VLAN so broadcast domains are smaller and easier to monitor","Add NAC posture checks that quarantine laptops lacking the current EDR version or missing critical operating system patches","Deploy an internal IPS between user subnets and the data center with signatures for known ransomware families"],"a":[0],"e":"The spread was workstation to workstation over admin protocols, so blocking those flows between endpoints cuts the path directly. Per-floor VLANs still allow routed traffic between them. Posture checks would not stop an already compliant device. An IPS between users and servers does not see peer-to-peer traffic within user subnets."},
{"id":"n53","d":3,"q":"A company's laptops use full-disk encryption where the key is released automatically by the device's security chip at boot, with no user input. A red team stole a powered-off laptop, captured the key in transit between the chip and the CPU during boot, and read the disk. The laptops hold sensitive designs. Help desk worries about extra boot steps for 8,000 users. Which control BEST addresses the finding?","o":["Turn on boot integrity and direct memory access protections so that boot-time hardware attacks through ports are blocked","Require a pre-boot PIN so the security chip releases the disk key only after the user proves knowledge of a secret","Add a remote wipe policy that triggers when a laptop is reported stolen or hasn't checked in for 14 days","Add file-level encryption on user folders on top of disk encryption so documents get a second layer of protection"],"a":[1],"e":"The weakness is that the key is released with nothing from the user, so whoever holds the device gets it. Adding a knowledge factor before release fixes that. The other boot protections address different attacks. A powered-off stolen laptop never receives a remote wipe. File-level encryption covers some folders but leaves the rest of the disk exposed the same way."},
{"id":"n54","d":3,"q":"An application tier runs as autoscaling containers that encrypt customer records. Today each container pulls a symmetric key from a secrets manager at startup and keeps it in memory. The architect wants to ensure a compromised container cannot take key material away for offline use and that misuse is limited and visible. The provider's KMS is HSM backed. Which design achieves this?","o":["Keep pulling the key from the secrets manager, but rotate it hourly so a copied key has a short useful life","Import key material generated on premises into the KMS so the company can revoke it from the provider at will","Bind the key to each container host's TPM so that attackers cannot copy it off the machine in a usable form for offline use","Call KMS to wrap and unwrap data keys, with policy scoped to the app role, per-tenant encryption context, and alerts"],"a":[3],"e":"With KMS-held keys, the KEK never leaves the HSM, the role can only call allowed operations, encryption context ties use to specific tenants, and alerts flag unusual call volume. A rotated key copied from memory still decrypts everything encrypted during its life. Imported key material helps with revocation but does not change how the container gets keys. TPM binding does not fit autoscaling containers that need a shared key."},
{"id":"n55","d":3,"q":"A multinational backhauls web traffic from 20,000 remote staff through proxies at its headquarters, and users in Asia and South America complain about performance. The HQ proxy hardware is due for refresh next year. The privacy office requires that web logs for EU staff stay in the EU. Security wants one policy regardless of location. Which design BEST improves performance while maintaining policy?","o":["Refresh the HQ proxies with higher capacity and add larger internet circuits at headquarters","Deploy proxy appliances in three regional hubs and point users to the nearest one with a geo-aware PAC file","A cloud SWG with nearby points of presence, one central policy, and log storage in the EU for EU staff","Split tunnel all internet traffic directly from laptops and enforce protective DNS filtering on the endpoints"],"a":[2],"e":"A cloud SWG puts inspection close to users with one policy, and regional log storage meets the residency need. Bigger HQ links do not fix distance-driven latency. Regional hubs help but add hardware to buy and manage in three places. DNS filtering alone drops full web inspection."},
{"id":"n56","d":3,"q":"A consumer device maker ships firmware updates to 2 million smart locks through a CDN. Images are signed with a key held in an HSM, and the bootloader verifies signatures. A competitor recently had its CDN account taken over, and attackers pushed an older, validly signed firmware with a known remote unlock bug. Which design addition MOST directly prevents that attack here?","o":["Pin the update server's TLS certificate in the device so it accepts updates only from the vendor's origin","Enforce anti-rollback with a monotonic version counter in protected storage that the bootloader checks","Encrypt firmware images with a device-specific key so attackers cannot read or analyze update content","Require the owner to approve each update in the mobile app before the lock downloads and installs it"],"a":[1],"e":"The attack used old but validly signed firmware, so signature checks pass. Anti-rollback with a hardware-backed version counter rejects anything older than the installed version. Certificate pinning protects the download path but a compromised origin or CDN path could still serve old images. Encryption protects confidentiality, not version. Owner approval would not tell users the update is a downgrade."},
{"id":"n57","d":3,"q":"In a cloud landing zone with 120 accounts, workload teams hold admin rights in their own accounts. The central API audit trail is created at the organization level and teams cannot modify it. However, an investigation found that a team had disabled VPC flow logs and DNS query logging in its account to cut costs. Which control MOST effectively prevents this?","o":["A configuration rule that detects disabled flow logs and re-enables them automatically within about ten minutes","Permission boundaries on team admin roles that exclude logging actions, attached by the account vending pipeline","Org-level preventive policies that deny changes to flow log and DNS log settings outside security roles","A monthly report to each team's director listing accounts with logging gaps and the estimated audit exposure"],"a":[2],"e":"Organization-level deny policies apply even to account admins, so teams cannot turn off the logs. Auto-remediation leaves gaps and reacts after the change. Permission boundaries help, but account admins can often create new roles without them unless something at the org level enforces it. Monthly reports detect problems long after the fact."},
{"id":"n58","d":3,"q":"A company needs 40 Gbps of steady throughput with consistent latency between its data center and a cloud region, and policy requires encryption in transit. Some legacy database replication traffic can't encrypt itself. The provider offers 100 Gbps dedicated connections with link-layer encryption, and its managed VPN is limited to about 1.25 Gbps per tunnel. Which design is MOST appropriate?","o":["Run the provider's managed VPN tunnels over the dedicated connection, adding more tunnels as throughput needs grow","Build internet VPN tunnels from the data center firewalls to a cloud firewall, spreading traffic across many tunnels","Use a dedicated connection with the provider's link-layer encryption enabled between the company edge and the provider","Use a dedicated connection without link encryption, and require application-level encryption on all traffic crossing it"],"a":[2],"e":"Link-layer encryption on a dedicated connection meets the throughput and latency need and covers traffic that can't encrypt itself. Stacking dozens of 1.25 Gbps tunnels is complex and fragile at 40 Gbps. Internet tunnels can't promise consistent latency. Requiring application encryption fails for the legacy replication traffic the scenario calls out."},
{"id":"n59","d":3,"q":"A critical customer web application has several confirmed injection and access control flaws found by a pen test. The vendor that built it is out of contract, and code fixes will take about four months to procure. The application sits behind a cloud load balancer that supports a managed WAF. Which control provides the FASTEST meaningful risk reduction?","o":["Deploy a RASP agent in the application runtime to block injection attempts from inside the running process","Deploy the managed WAF with core rules plus custom rules for the known flaws, tuned briefly in detect mode","Run an authenticated DAST scan every week and send findings to the vendor procurement team for prioritization","Add network IPS signatures at the perimeter firewall to block common injection patterns in HTTP traffic"],"a":[1],"e":"A WAF on the existing load balancer can be enabled in days, and custom rules can virtually patch the specific confirmed flaws. RASP needs runtime changes and testing in an app no one can currently support. More scanning finds issues but does not reduce risk. A perimeter IPS often cannot see inside TLS and lacks app-specific context."},
{"id":"n60","d":3,"q":"A messaging service's design has clients encrypt a random session key with the server's long-term RSA-3072 public key using OAEP padding. The server key lives in an HSM, and the threat model includes a nation-state that records traffic and might later obtain the key through legal pressure. The product team says OAEP fixed the padding issues found in last year's review. What is the MOST significant weakness?","o":["OAEP is still open to Bleichenbacher-style padding oracles, so an attacker can decrypt session keys online","RSA decryption is slower than elliptic-curve key agreement, which limits how many sessions the HSM can handle","Key transport does not authenticate the server, so a man-in-the-middle can substitute a different public key","If the server key is later obtained, all recorded sessions can be decrypted because there is no forward secrecy"],"a":[3],"e":"With key transport, every session key is encrypted under one long-term key, so its later disclosure exposes all recorded traffic. Ephemeral key agreement would prevent that. Bleichenbacher attacks target PKCS#1 v1.5, not OAEP used correctly. Performance is a scaling concern, not a security weakness. Clients encrypt to a known server public key, which binds the session to the server if the key is validated."},
{"id":"n61","d":3,"q":"A water utility's SCADA system uses Modbus/TCP between HMIs and about 80 PLCs. The PLCs cannot be upgraded for at least five years and do not support TLS, IPsec, or 802.1X. Engineering workstations need write access during maintenance windows; otherwise, only reads are needed. The utility also plans a new billing system next year. Which compensating control is MOST effective?","o":["Zones and conduits with a Modbus-aware firewall limiting writes to engineering hosts in windows, plus passive OT monitoring","Upgrade the HMIs to Modbus/TCP Security with TLS so that traffic from the HMIs is encrypted and authenticated","Add IPsec gateways in front of each group of PLCs so traffic from the HMIs is tunneled to the controllers","Enable 802.1X with MAB on the OT switches so only known PLC and HMI MAC addresses can connect to the control network"],"a":[0],"e":"Protocol-aware filtering restricts Modbus function codes by source and time, and passive monitoring detects misuse, all without touching the PLCs. Modbus/TCP Security needs support on both ends. IPsec gateways protect transit but still let any host on the inside issue writes. MAB is easily spoofed and does not control what commands are sent. The billing system is noise."},
{"id":"n62","d":3,"q":"A manufacturer already enforces DMARC p=reject on its own domain. Last month accounts payable paid a fake invoice after an email thread from a long-standing supplier asked to update bank details. Investigation showed the supplier's real mailbox was compromised, and the messages passed SPF, DKIM, and DMARC. Which control is MOST effective against a repeat?","o":["Register common look-alike variants of the company's and its top suppliers' domains to prevent attackers from using them","Add external sender warning banners and train AP staff to check sender addresses carefully before acting","Ask the supplier to move from p=quarantine to p=reject on its domain and verify the change with DNS lookups","Require out-of-band verification of bank detail changes using known contacts, plus mailbox anomaly detection"],"a":[3],"e":"The messages came from the supplier's real, authenticated mailbox, so domain controls do not help. Verifying payment changes through a known phone contact breaks the fraud, and anomaly detection can flag unusual threads. Look-alike registration addresses a different attack. Banners and sender checks fail when the sender is genuine. The supplier's DMARC policy is irrelevant when the mail is legitimately authenticated."},
{"id":"n63","d":3,"q":"An architect is mapping a vendor's zero trust product to NIST SP 800-207. The product has a cloud service that scores each request using identity, device posture, and threat intelligence; a control service that issues short-lived session tokens and tells gateways to open or close sessions; and gateways in front of apps. Which product component corresponds to the policy engine?","o":["The gateways in front of the apps, which allow, monitor, and end the connections to each resource","The control service that issues session tokens and tells the gateways to open or close each session","The threat intelligence feed and device posture data that are consumed by the scoring service","The cloud service that scores each request with its trust algorithm and decides to grant or deny"],"a":[3],"e":"In SP 800-207 the policy engine runs the trust algorithm and makes the grant or deny decision. The policy administrator acts on that decision by setting up or tearing down the path, like the control service here. Gateways are policy enforcement points. Posture and threat feeds are data sources that inform the engine."},
{"id":"n64","d":3,"q":"A data center upgrade replaces water sprinklers in the main hall with a clean agent gas system. The hall is staffed around the clock by operators, and contractors work under the raised floor several times a month. The facilities manager is focused on avoiding equipment damage from discharge and getting the system certified before year end. Which consideration is MOST important for personnel safety?","o":["Very early smoke detection under the raised floor so incipient fires are found before suppression is needed","Integration with the emergency power off system so all equipment in the hall shuts down automatically before agent discharge","Pre-discharge alarms, abort switches, evacuation drills that include contractors, and an agent safe at design levels","Choosing an agent with low ozone depletion and global warming potential to meet environmental regulations"],"a":[2],"e":"People in the room need warning time, a way to stop discharge, practiced evacuation, and an agent concentration that is safe if exposure occurs. Early detection and EPO integration protect equipment and continuity. Environmental impact matters for compliance, not occupant safety."},
{"id":"n65","d":3,"q":"An insurer found 37 long-lived cloud access keys with full admin rights, created two years ago by developers and embedded in scripts on their laptops. Some scripts run nightly data pipelines whose owners are unclear. There is no evidence of compromise. Leadership wants the keys gone this quarter without breaking production jobs. What should the architect do FIRST?","o":["Delete all 37 keys immediately and let teams request new access through the standard role request process","Rotate each key and move the new keys into a secrets manager so that they are kept off developer laptops","Use key last-used data and audit logs to map what each key does, then deactivate unused keys and plan replacements","Deploy secret scanning on developer laptops and repositories to find each place the keys are stored today"],"a":[2],"e":"Without knowing which keys support production jobs, any bulk action risks outages. Last-used data and audit logs show what each key does, unused keys can be safely deactivated, and active ones can move to federated short-lived roles. Deleting everything breaks pipelines. Rotation keeps long-lived admin keys in place. Secret scanning finds copies but does not reveal usage."},
{"id":"n66","d":3,"q":"A new regional office will run VoIP, guest Wi-Fi, building management systems, and corporate devices on one set of switches and access points to save cost. The facilities vendor manages the BMS remotely and asks for a flat network to simplify its support. The budget does not include a separate physical network. Which design gives the MOST risk reduction on the shared hardware?","o":["Separate VLANs or VRFs per trust zone, with traffic between zones forced through a firewall with explicit rules","Enable 802.1X on all wired ports and WPA3-Enterprise on the corporate SSID so that only known, authenticated devices connect","Provide the BMS vendor with a site-to-site VPN that terminates directly on the BMS controller subnet","Deploy an IDS on a SPAN port of the core switch to watch for attacks moving between device types"],"a":[0],"e":"Logical segmentation with a firewall between zones limits what a compromised guest, phone, or BMS device can reach, on the same hardware. 802.1X controls who connects but does not separate zones. A vendor VPN into the BMS subnet is needed later but adds exposure if the network is flat. An IDS only detects."},
{"id":"n67","d":3,"q":"A payment processor is selecting HSMs for PIN translation and key exchange with card networks. The infrastructure team prefers a general-purpose HSM model it already runs for code signing, which carries a government crypto validation and a standard API. The card networks will audit the deployment next year. Which property is MOST important for this use?","o":["Very high signing throughput, so the HSM can handle peak card transaction volume without queuing","Payment industry HSM approval with native payment functions such as PIN translation and key exchange","Reuse of the existing general-purpose HSM model, so operations has only one platform to support","General government crypto validation with a standard API, so common crypto libraries can call it from payment applications"],"a":[1],"e":"Card networks audit against payment industry HSM requirements, and PIN translation needs functions general-purpose HSMs don't provide. Throughput matters but doesn't make a device compliant. Reuse is an operational preference, not a requirement. General crypto validation is necessary but not sufficient for payment use."},
{"id":"n68","d":3,"q":"Several internal TLS certificates expired last quarter and caused outages. Certificates are tracked in a spreadsheet by the network team, but app teams buy some directly from a public CA. Public TLS certificate lifetimes are shrinking under CA/Browser Forum rules, toward 47 days by 2029. The CIO asks for the change that BEST addresses the root cause.","o":["Automated discovery, a central inventory with owners, and ACME-based renewal and deployment with expiry monitoring","Issue internal certificates with three-year lifetimes from the private CA to reduce how often renewals come due across teams","Assign a named owner to each certificate in the spreadsheet with calendar reminders 60 days before expiry","Replace individual certificates with a small set of wildcard certificates managed by the network team"],"a":[0],"e":"The root cause is manual tracking that misses certificates. Discovery finds everything, and automated renewal scales as lifetimes shrink. Longer internal lifetimes do not apply to public certificates and increase exposure if keys leak. Owners and reminders keep the manual process that failed. Wildcards spread one key across many systems."},
{"id":"n69","d":3,"q":"A health system is hosting a patient portal in a public cloud under a signed business associate agreement. The project manager's slide says that with the BAA in place, HIPAA compliance for the portal is 'covered by the provider.' The portal uses managed databases, object storage, and a third-party analytics script. Which statement is MOST accurate?","o":["The BAA moves Security Rule risk analysis duties to the provider for the portal systems that it hosts","The provider becomes responsible for breaches of PHI that result from customer misconfigurations","Using HIPAA-eligible services under the BAA makes the portal compliant without further action","The health system still owns its configuration, access control, and risk analysis for PHI in the portal"],"a":[3],"e":"A BAA makes the provider responsible for safeguards in its part of the service, but the customer still configures services, controls access, and performs its own risk analysis. Risk analysis duties do not transfer. Misconfiguration breaches are the customer's. Eligible services still need correct configuration. The analytics script may need its own review."},
{"id":"n70","d":3,"q":"A global retailer runs its site active-active in two cloud regions. During a failover test, the second region served traffic without the latest WAF rules and with secrets that had been rotated in the first region but not replicated. Customers were not affected. Leadership wants to add a third region next year. Which design change BEST supports both availability and security?","o":["Switch to active-passive with a warm standby region so that only one region at a time needs fully current security controls","Add global load balancer health checks that fail traffic over only when the WAF reports itself as healthy","Deploy WAF rules, secrets, and other controls to all regions from one pipeline, with drift checks and failover tests","Run the third region in a different cloud provider so a single provider outage cannot take the site offline"],"a":[2],"e":"The failure was security drift between regions. Deploying controls as code from one pipeline to every region, checking drift, and testing failover keeps them identical. Active-passive still needs the standby to be current. Health checks confirm the WAF is running, not that its rules are current. A second provider adds resilience but more drift risk."},
{"id":"n71","d":3,"q":"A company gives offshore contractors access to source code only through VDI. Contractors use their own unmanaged laptops, and the VDI policy blocks clipboard, drive mapping, and printing. The vendor management office says VDI resolves the main data risks. Which risk does the VDI design address LEAST?","o":["Source code being stored on contractor laptop disks after a session ends","Code copied out of a session through the clipboard into a local editor","Malware on a contractor laptop capturing screens and keystrokes in session","Contractor desktops running unpatched software that IT cannot update"],"a":[2],"e":"VDI keeps data and desktops in the data center, so it handles local storage, clipboard controls, and patching of the virtual desktops. It cannot stop a compromised endpoint from capturing what is shown on screen or typed, which is the risk left largely open."},
{"id":"n72","d":3,"q":"A SOC is evaluating network detection and response for an environment where most traffic uses TLS 1.3, and several SaaS providers have enabled Encrypted Client Hello. The company does not plan to decrypt traffic. An analyst suggests relying on certificate details seen in handshakes to spot malicious servers. Which capability remains MOST useful?","o":["Inspecting server certificates in the handshake to flag self-signed or newly issued certificates on servers","Categorizing destinations by the SNI value in each Client Hello and alerting on known malicious domains","Passive decryption using copies of server private keys loaded onto the NDR sensors for internal services","Metadata and behavior analytics such as flow patterns, JA4 fingerprints, beaconing, and DNS anomalies"],"a":[3],"e":"Flow behavior, client fingerprints, timing, and DNS remain visible without decryption. TLS 1.3 encrypts the server certificate, so handshake certificate inspection no longer works. ECH hides the real SNI for supporting services. TLS 1.3 uses ephemeral key exchange, so server private keys alone cannot decrypt sessions."},
{"id":"n73","d":3,"q":"A call center database holds customer national ID numbers. Agents should see only the last four digits, while fraud investigators need full numbers. Besides the CRM screens, agents use an ad hoc reporting tool that lets them write their own filters against the same database. The DBA proposes the database's built-in dynamic data masking because it needs no schema changes. Which design BEST implements the requirement?","o":["Role-based dynamic data masking on the ID column, showing agents only the last four digits in query results","Masking in the CRM user interface for agent roles, with investigators shown the full number in a separate screen","Transparent data encryption on the database, with investigators granted access through a separate reporting role","Store the last four digits in a separate column, and grant agents only views without the full ID column"],"a":[3],"e":"Agents can write their own filters, and dynamic masking only changes what is displayed, so filters on the masked column can infer full values. Removing the full column from what agents can query closes that path. UI masking is bypassed by the reporting tool. TDE protects files at rest, not query results."},
{"id":"n74","d":3,"q":"A bank's CISO wants to be able to replace cryptographic algorithms quickly as post-quantum standards mature. Crypto is spread across mainframe apps, Java services, network devices, HSMs, and vendor products. The architecture board wants a plan by next quarter, and a vendor is pitching a crypto agility platform. What should the architect do FIRST?","o":["Build a cryptographic inventory showing where each algorithm, key, and library is used, and who owns it","Require post-quantum algorithms in all new procurement contracts starting at the next renewal cycle","Move applications to a central crypto service so algorithms can be changed in one place by configuration","Replace RSA with elliptic curve algorithms across the estate to reduce key sizes ahead of the PQ migration"],"a":[0],"e":"Agility starts with knowing where crypto lives and who owns it, otherwise no change can be planned or tested. Procurement clauses and a central service are good next steps that depend on that inventory. Moving to ECC is not a post-quantum improvement and creates a second migration."},
{"id":"n75","d":3,"q":"A company's corporate Wi-Fi authenticates users with directory passwords inside a tunneled protocol. Laptops are configured through device management, but an audit found many of them don't verify the authentication server's identity. In a red team test, rogue access points captured the password exchanges and the hashes cracked offline. The company has a NAC platform and an internal PKI. Which change provides the STRONGEST protection against this attack?","o":["Enforce server identity verification on every laptop through device management, while keeping password-based network login","Move to mutual certificate-based authentication with device certificates issued through device management, verifying the server too","Deploy wireless intrusion prevention to detect and contain rogue access points that broadcast the corporate network name","Move the corporate network to a modern shared-passphrase mode, and have device management rotate that passphrase on every laptop each quarter"],"a":[1],"e":"Removing the reusable secret is the strongest fix. With mutual certificate authentication there is no password exchange to capture and crack, and verifying the server blocks the rogue AP. Enforcing server verification helps but still leaves passwords in play if a laptop is misconfigured or a user clicks through a prompt. Wireless IPS is detective and has coverage gaps. A shared passphrase is weaker for an enterprise network and gives no per-user identity."},
{"id":"n76","d":3,"q":"A company wants to block malicious domains for 6,000 managed laptops, including when users work from home and hotels. Today filtering exists only on the internal resolvers. Users have complained about the always-on VPN's performance, and some browsers are set to use public DNS over HTTPS by default. Which design is MOST effective?","o":["Force all laptops into a persistent full-tunnel VPN so their DNS goes through the internal filtered resolvers","Push an endpoint DNS agent or DoH configuration to a protective resolver, and disable browser DoH by policy","Add more threat feeds to the internal resolvers and require users to connect to VPN before browsing","Allow browser DoH to a reputable public resolver that offers malware filtering for all of its users"],"a":[1],"e":"An endpoint-enforced protective resolver works on and off network, and disabling browser DoH stops the bypass. Full-tunnel VPN works but brings back the performance complaints. Better feeds on internal resolvers still miss off-network traffic if users skip the VPN. Letting browsers choose their own resolver loses central policy and logging."},
{"id":"n77","d":3,"q":"A company runs 5,000 Linux servers built from a golden image. Auditors found that many servers no longer match the hardening baseline months after build because of manual fixes during incidents. The auditors want evidence of continuous compliance. The ops team already uses Ansible for application deployment. Which approach provides the MOST consistent and auditable results?","o":["Rebuild the golden image monthly with the latest CIS hardening and redeploy servers on a rolling schedule","Run hardening scripts during provisioning and require a change ticket for manual configuration changes made later","Define the baseline as code in Ansible, apply it continuously, and scan for drift with tracked exceptions","Perform quarterly manual audits of a server sample and fix deviations found during each of those reviews"],"a":[2],"e":"Applying the baseline continuously as code and scanning for drift corrects and records deviations, which gives auditors ongoing evidence. Monthly image rebuilds help but leave drift between rebuilds. Build-time scripts plus tickets depend on people avoiding manual changes, which already failed. Quarterly samples are slow and incomplete."},
{"id":"n78","d":3,"q":"A university's researchers move multi-terabyte datasets with partners over 100 Gbps links with long round-trip times. Transfers through the campus firewall cluster run at a fraction of link speed, and packet captures show drops in the firewall's buffers that collapse TCP throughput. Researchers are threatening to buy their own internet circuits. Which architecture is MOST appropriate?","o":["Buy a larger firewall cluster rated for 100 Gbps of throughput with deep packet inspection still enabled","Move research data transfers to a public cloud storage service and have partners download from the cloud","Place research workstations on a separate network outside the firewall with host-based firewalls on each one","A Science DMZ with dedicated transfer nodes, stateless router ACLs, and passive monitoring of the flows"],"a":[3],"e":"A Science DMZ puts tuned transfer nodes on a simple path with stateless ACLs and passive monitoring, avoiding the firewall buffers that collapse TCP. A bigger firewall may still drop bursts from long fat flows. Cloud staging adds cost and steps. Moving general workstations outside the firewall exposes far more than the dedicated transfer nodes."},
{"id":"n79","d":3,"q":"A company already requires MFA with push notifications for all SaaS apps through its cloud IdP. Attackers recently used an adversary-in-the-middle phishing kit to steal session cookies after MFA and replayed them from their own machines. Employees use corporate laptops and some personal phones. Which control is MOST effective against this attack?","o":["Shorten SaaS session lifetimes to one hour so stolen cookies expire before they can be put to use","Turn on number matching for push MFA so users must type a code shown on the login screen to approve","Require compliant managed devices plus phishing-resistant MFA, with token binding where it is supported","Restrict SaaS sign-ins to corporate and home IP ranges that each employee registers in a self-service portal"],"a":[2],"e":"Requiring a managed, compliant device means a stolen token replayed from the attacker's machine fails the device check, and FIDO2 resists the proxy phishing itself. Short sessions narrow the window but cookies are used within minutes. Number matching stops push fatigue, not proxying. IP restrictions are hard to maintain and can be bypassed with residential proxies."},
{"id":"n80","d":3,"q":"A broker-dealer must keep trade audit logs for seven years and prove to the regulator that no record was altered or deleted. A FINRA exam is scheduled for the spring, and the CFO wants storage costs held flat. Logs land in a cloud object store and are also indexed in the SIEM for 90 days. A senior storage admin has full rights in the cloud account. Which design is BEST?","o":["Object lock in compliance mode for seven years, plus hash chains or signed digests verified on a schedule","Object lock in governance mode with a seven-year retention, limited to the security team's bypass role","Keep the logs in the SIEM for seven years with role-based access and audit logging of administrator actions","Copy logs weekly to tape stored at an offsite vault, with chain of custody forms completed for each tape that is shipped"],"a":[0],"e":"Compliance mode prevents deletion or shortening retention even by privileged users, and hash chains or signed digests prove integrity. Governance mode can be bypassed by a privileged role. A SIEM with RBAC still lets admins change data. Tape with custody forms shows handling but not that content is unchanged."},
{"id":"n81","d":3,"q":"A payment team deploys cloud functions as zip packages on the provider's managed Node.js runtime. Functions use an IAM role, pull secrets from a vault, and process card data from an API gateway. A QSA assessment is due in March. During a review, the team asks which item on the security checklist is NOT their responsibility.","o":["Patching the operating system and Node.js runtime underneath the functions","Updating npm packages that are bundled into each function's deployment zip","Scoping the function's execution role to the minimum permissions needed","Validating input that arrives from the API gateway before processing it"],"a":[0],"e":"With a managed runtime delivered as zip packages, the provider patches the OS and language runtime. The customer still owns bundled dependencies, the function's role permissions, and input validation in its own code. If the team deployed container images instead, runtime patching would shift back to them."},
{"id":"n82","d":3,"q":"A pipeline operator's control system vendors need remote access a few times a month. Today, one vendor has an always-on cellular modem attached to a PLC network switch, and another uses a site-to-site VPN from its own office. A new regulation requires the operator to approve, monitor, and be able to end vendor sessions. Which design MOST effectively limits risk?","o":["A jump host in the industrial DMZ with MFA that vendors can use whenever they need access to OT assets","Brokered vendor sessions through a PAM gateway in the DMZ with per-request approval, recording, and time limits","Keep the site-to-site VPN but restrict it with firewall rules to only the specific OT assets that the vendor supports","Replace the cellular modem with a vendor-managed remote access appliance placed on the OT network"],"a":[1],"e":"Brokered sessions with approval, recording, and time limits match the regulation and stop standing access. A jump host with MFA allows access at any time and lacks approval and recording. A filtered VPN still trusts the vendor's whole network. A vendor-managed appliance on the OT network keeps a path the operator cannot fully control."},
{"id":"n83","d":3,"q":"A new headquarters has about 2,000 staff arriving between 8 and 9 a.m. through the lobby, and a data hall on the third floor. Fire code requires free egress, and the lobby must accommodate wheelchair users. A tailgating test got testers into both the lobby and the data hall. Which design BEST prevents tailgating while meeting the constraints?","o":["Two-door mantraps at each lobby entry so that only one person at a time can pass into the building","Badge readers with anti-passback on all doors so that a badge cannot be reused to let a second person in","Optical turnstiles with tailgate detection and an accessible lane in the lobby, plus a single-person portal at the data hall","A security guard at the lobby during business hours and CCTV with analytics covering the data hall entrance"],"a":[2],"e":"Optical turnstiles handle high throughput with an accessible lane and detect tailgaters, and a single-person portal gives strict control at the data hall. Lobby mantraps cannot handle 2,000 people in an hour and complicate accessibility. Anti-passback stops badge sharing, not someone following through. Guards and cameras help but do not physically prevent tailgating at the data hall."},
{"id":"n84","d":3,"q":"A Kubernetes platform pulls images only from a private registry. An attacker with stolen CI credentials pushed a malicious image under an existing release tag, and the cluster deployed it during a routine restart. The pipeline already runs vulnerability scans, and the image passed. Which control MOST directly prevents this from happening again?","o":["Restrict nodes so they can pull images only from the private registry over a private network endpoint","Sign images in the release pipeline and verify signer identity and digest in an admission policy","Make release tags immutable in the registry so existing tags cannot be overwritten by a new push","Raise the vulnerability scan threshold so images with high findings cannot be pushed to the registry"],"a":[1],"e":"Admission checks that require a signature from the release pipeline's identity reject images pushed with stolen CI credentials outside that pipeline, and pinning digests stops tag swaps. Registry restrictions already existed. Immutable tags help but the attacker could push a new tag. A malicious image can pass vulnerability scanning."},
{"id":"n85","d":3,"q":"All 9,000 laptops use an always-on, full-tunnel VPN so the data center proxies can inspect web traffic. Teams and Zoom calls suffer from jitter, and the VPN concentrators run near capacity during the day. The network team proposes split tunneling all internet traffic. The security team does not want to lose web inspection. What is the BEST approach?","o":["Split tunnel all internet traffic and rely on endpoint EDR and DNS filtering for protection off the tunnel","Add VPN concentrator capacity and QoS for real-time media so that calls perform better within the tunnel","Exclude only collaboration media traffic by the vendors' published IP ranges, keeping other traffic tunneled","Move all users to ZTNA immediately and retire both the VPN and the data center proxies in the same project phase"],"a":[2],"e":"Targeted exclusion of high-volume, low-risk media traffic relieves the tunnel and fixes call quality while web traffic is still inspected. Full split tunnel drops web inspection. More capacity and QoS cannot fix the latency added by hairpinning. A rushed ZTNA migration is a much larger change than the problem needs."},
{"id":"n86","d":3,"q":"During a live sports stream, attackers sent slow, legitimate-looking HTTPS requests to the login endpoint from about 80,000 residential IPs, exhausting backend database connections. Video segments were served from the CDN and were fine. The origin already sits behind an upstream BGP scrubbing contract, which did not trigger. Which control is MOST effective?","o":["Autoscale the login tier and enlarge the database connection pool so the backend can absorb the request surge","Activate the BGP scrubbing service manually during events so all traffic to the origin passes through scrubbing","Lower server timeouts and set a per-IP connection limit on the origin load balancer for the login endpoint","Route login through the CDN's WAF so full requests are buffered, rate limited per endpoint, and challenged"],"a":[3],"e":"An application-layer proxy terminates slow requests before they reach the origin, and endpoint rate limits and challenges filter bots. Autoscaling raises cost and the database remains the bottleneck. Network scrubbing targets volumetric attacks and does not see HTTP behavior inside TLS. Per-IP limits fail against 80,000 addresses sending little each."},
{"id":"n87","d":3,"q":"A company sends nightly backups to a third-party cloud storage provider. Policy states the provider must not be able to read backup data under any circumstances, including a legal demand served on the provider. The provider offers server-side encryption with its own KMS, customer-managed keys in that KMS, and customer-provided keys sent with each request. Which design meets the policy?","o":["Server-side encryption with customer-managed keys in the provider's KMS, with key policy limited to the backup role","Server-side encryption with customer-provided keys sent with each request and not stored by the provider","Client-side encryption before upload, with keys held in the company's own KMS or HSM on premises","Transfer over a private interconnect with TLS 1.3 and server-side encryption using the provider's default keys"],"a":[2],"e":"Only client-side encryption keeps keys and plaintext away from the provider. Customer-managed keys still sit in the provider's KMS, which the provider operates. Customer-provided keys are sent to the provider with each request, so it handles them briefly. Transport security and default keys do not prevent provider access."},
{"id":"n88","d":3,"q":"A shared Kubernetes cluster hosts several product teams in separate namespaces. One team's compromised pod reached another team's database pods. The platform team had already applied default-deny NetworkPolicies in every namespace months ago. The cluster uses Flannel as its CNI. A service mesh is installed but database pods are not part of it. Which action MOST directly prevents this?","o":["Re-apply default-deny NetworkPolicies with explicit namespace selectors and test them using a policy linter","Add service mesh authorization policies so only the owning team's services can call its database service","Move to a CNI that enforces NetworkPolicy, such as Calico or Cilium, and validate the default-deny policies","Split each team into its own cluster so that no network path exists between different teams' workloads at all"],"a":[2],"e":"Flannel does not enforce NetworkPolicy, so the existing policies had no effect. A CNI that enforces them makes the default-deny rules real. Rewriting policies does nothing on Flannel. Mesh authorization does not cover pods outside the mesh. Separate clusters work but are a much larger change than needed."},
{"id":"n89","d":3,"q":"A security review finds database passwords in environment variables defined in deployment files that have been in a Git repository for three years. About 60 developers and two contractors have had read access. The team plans to move to a secrets manager with workload identity next quarter. What should the architect recommend FIRST?","o":["Rotate the exposed database passwords now and treat them as compromised, then migrate to the secrets manager","Rewrite the Git history to purge the passwords from all commits and force everyone to re-clone the repository","Enable secret scanning in the repository so that new commits containing credentials are blocked on push","Start the secrets manager migration now so passwords are injected at runtime instead of being stored in files"],"a":[0],"e":"The passwords have been readable by many people for years, so they must be treated as compromised and rotated before anything else. Purging history does not undo existing clones. Secret scanning prevents new leaks but not old ones. The migration is the right long-term fix, but until the passwords change they remain usable."},
{"id":"n90","d":3,"q":"An architect learns that the SD-WAN orchestrator managing 300 branch edges is reachable from the internet with single-factor local accounts. The edges keep forwarding if they lose contact with the orchestrator, and data plane tunnels use keys negotiated between edges. Which risk is MOST significant?","o":["An attacker could map the branch topology and addressing to plan attacks against specific sites","An attacker could push configuration or policy to every edge, such as rerouting or opening traffic","An attacker could take the orchestrator offline, causing branches to lose connectivity until restored","An attacker could decrypt branch tunnel traffic by reading keys negotiated between edge devices"],"a":[1],"e":"The orchestrator's power is pushing configuration to every edge, so an attacker can change routing, open firewall rules, or disable security fleet-wide. Topology mapping is serious but less damaging. Edges keep forwarding if the orchestrator goes down. Tunnel keys are negotiated between edges, so the orchestrator does not normally hold them."},
{"id":"n91","d":3,"q":"A law firm uses a SaaS collaboration platform, and staff often share documents with clients and co-counsel. Twice this year, privileged documents were shared with the wrong external party by mistake. Partners will not accept a block on external sharing. Many lawyers use sync clients on managed laptops, and some use personal tablets through the browser. Which approach is MOST effective?","o":["Disable external sharing tenant-wide and route all client document exchange through a separate secure portal","Auto-label privileged content, use platform DLP to block or justify external shares, and expire the guest links","Route all platform traffic through an inline CASB proxy that inspects and blocks risky uploads and external shares","Require annual training on privileged document handling and a sharing checklist for paralegals and partners"],"a":[1],"e":"Labels and DLP enforced inside the platform apply no matter which client or device is used, and they target privileged content while allowing normal sharing. A tenant-wide block is ruled out by the partners. An inline proxy misses sync clients and some device paths, and sharing happens in the SaaS API. Training helps but mistakes already happened."},
{"id":"n92","d":3,"q":"A new data center is being built dual stack. The firewall team's rule base and change process cover only IPv4, and servers have IPv6 enabled by default with SLAAC. A server admin suggests disabling IPv6 in the OS registry on all Windows servers to keep things simple. The go-live date is in three months. Which consideration is MOST important?","o":["Disable IPv6 on all servers through policy so that only the reviewed IPv4 rule base controls traffic","Rely on IPsec, which is part of the IPv6 standard, to secure traffic between all data center servers","Use NAT66 at the edge so internal IPv6 addresses are hidden as IPv4 addresses are today","Write IPv6 rules that match the IPv4 policy and enable first-hop protections such as RA Guard"],"a":[3],"e":"Hosts will use IPv6 whether or not the firewall team plans for it, so policy parity and first-hop protections against rogue router advertisements are needed. Microsoft advises against disabling IPv6 on Windows, and doing so can break components. IPsec is optional in IPv6 and is not configured automatically. NAT66 is uncommon and does not replace filtering."},
{"id":"n93","d":3,"q":"A company's backup design keeps production data, a copy on a local backup appliance, and a cloud copy synced from the appliance with object versioning. The appliance and cloud account share admin credentials. Restores are tested only when someone needs a file. The CIO asks how the design compares with the 3-2-1-1-0 rule. Which change BEST closes the gaps?","o":["Add a fourth copy on a second disk array in another building on the same campus as the data center","Make the offsite copy immutable under separate credentials and add automated restore testing","Replace the cloud copy with weekly tape so the second media type is a different technology","Raise the cloud versioning retention period from 30 to 90 days so older copies are kept for longer"],"a":[1],"e":"The rule needs one immutable or air-gapped copy and zero restore errors, which means verified restores. The cloud copy shares credentials and is a sync target, so it is not truly immutable. Another onsite copy does not fix those gaps. Tape adds media diversity but not testing. Longer versioning helps but versions can still be deleted with shared admin credentials."},
{"id":"n94","d":3,"q":"A threat hunt found a host sending long, random-looking subdomain queries to an unfamiliar domain every few seconds. Endpoints use internal resolvers, but the firewall allows outbound UDP 53 and TCP 853 to the internet, and some browsers use DoH. The mail team relies on TXT lookups for SPF and DKIM. Which control is MOST effective?","o":["Force DNS through internal resolvers by blocking other DNS, DoT, and known DoH, then analyze query patterns","Block TXT record queries at the internal resolvers, since tunneling tools often encode payloads in TXT records","Enable DNSSEC validation on the internal resolvers so that forged responses from attacker domains are rejected","Add IPS signatures for common tunneling tools such as iodine and dnscat2 at the network perimeter"],"a":[0],"e":"Tunneling is only visible if all DNS goes through resolvers you watch, then query length, entropy, and volume reveal it. Blocking TXT breaks mail authentication and tunnels can use other record types. DNSSEC checks authenticity, not intent, and unsigned zones are still resolved. Tool signatures miss custom or modified tools."},
{"id":"n95","d":3,"q":"A defense contractor has decided to deploy a high-interaction honeynet inside its network to study attackers who get past the perimeter. It will mimic engineering servers. Legal counsel has raised concerns about liability. The SOC also plans to seed fake credentials in Active Directory next year. Which design consideration is MOST important for the honeynet?","o":["Make the honeynet look realistic by copying real engineering data so attackers stay engaged longer","Place the honeynet in the internet DMZ so it attracts more attackers and produces more intelligence","Contain outbound traffic so the honeynet cannot be used to attack others, and capture all activity","Give the honeynet hosts trust relationships with production AD so that they look like real domain members to attackers"],"a":[2],"e":"A compromised high-interaction honeynet can launch attacks on production or third parties, which is also the main liability concern, so data control with full capture is key. Real engineering data puts sensitive information at risk. A DMZ placement studies a different threat than post-perimeter attackers. Trust with production AD gives attackers a path into the real domain."},
{"id":"n96","d":3,"q":"A company deploys all cloud infrastructure through Terraform pipelines that run policy-as-code checks. During incidents, engineers sometimes make changes in the console using break-glass roles, and one such change left a database publicly reachable for two weeks. The CISO wants insecure configurations kept out of production. Which control MOST effectively achieves this?","o":["Require two peer reviewers on each Terraform pull request before merge into the main branch","Org-level preventive policies blocking the riskiest settings everywhere, alongside the pipeline checks","Run CSPM scans hourly and open high-priority tickets for each public database that it detects","Remove console write access so that all changes, including emergency fixes, go through the pipelines"],"a":[1],"e":"Preventive policies at the organization level apply to console and pipeline changes alike, so the riskiest settings cannot be created even during break-glass use. Peer review applies only to pipeline changes. Hourly CSPM is detective. Removing console access can block incident response and is often impractical."},
{"id":"n97","d":3,"q":"A partner needs to call one internal order API from its own systems. Its network security is unknown, and its IT team has asked for a site-to-site VPN because that is how it connects to other customers. The API is internal-only today and has no authentication of its own. The partner expects to start sending orders next month. Which design is BEST?","o":["Build the site-to-site VPN and restrict it with ACLs so the partner's subnets reach only the API server","Publish the API through a WAF with a shared API key per partner and IP allowlisting at the WAF","Set up private cloud peering with the partner and route only the API's subnet over it","Expose only that API through a gateway with mTLS or OAuth client credentials and rate limits"],"a":[3],"e":"An API gateway exposes only the required API and adds strong client authentication, input validation, and rate limits, without trusting the partner's network. A VPN with ACLs gives network-level access from an unknown network to an unauthenticated API. A shared API key is weak authentication. Peering is still network-level trust."},
{"id":"n98","d":3,"q":"A financial application runs behind a cloud load balancer with a WAF that must inspect HTTP content. Compliance requires encryption in transit on every hop, including inside the VPC. The provider says it encrypts traffic between its facilities. The team's first draft used TLS passthrough to the backends. What is the BEST approach?","o":["TLS passthrough to the backends so traffic is encrypted end to end, with the WAF moved onto each web server","Terminate TLS at the load balancer, then re-encrypt to backends that present certificates the LB validates","Terminate TLS at the load balancer and send plaintext to backends, since the provider encrypts its links","Terminate TLS at the load balancer and use client certificate authentication for each external user"],"a":[1],"e":"Terminating at the load balancer lets the WAF inspect traffic, and re-encrypting with validated backend certificates meets the every-hop requirement. Passthrough blinds the managed WAF and pushes inspection to each server. Provider link encryption does not cover every hop inside the VPC. Client certificates for users do not address backend encryption."},
{"id":"n99","d":3,"q":"A research lab's server room has one air conditioning unit and one UPS. The BIA rates the systems as critical, with an RTO of four hours. The servers control on-site lab instruments and cannot be moved or replicated off site. Last summer the AC failed on a weekend and nobody noticed until Monday. Which design change BEST addresses environmental risk?","o":["Install environmental sensors with alerts to the on-call engineer for temperature and humidity changes","Replace the UPS with a larger single unit that gives several hours of runtime during utility power outages","Replicate the critical servers to a cloud DR region with automated failover inside the four-hour RTO","N+1 cooling and power, including a generator, with environmental monitoring and alerting to on-call staff"],"a":[3],"e":"Redundant cooling and power remove the single points of failure, and monitoring makes failures visible. Sensors alone would have alerted sooner but a single AC failure would still take the room down. A larger single UPS remains a single point of failure. Cloud DR is ruled out because the servers must stay on site."},
{"id":"n100","d":3,"q":"A government agency is choosing a signing approach for permits that must be verifiable for 20 years. Signing certificates last two years, and the issuing CA will likely be replaced at least once in that time. Long-term policy also requires planning for post-quantum risk. The current platform signs with RSA-3072. Which approach is MOST appropriate?","o":["Move to larger RSA keys now so the permit signatures stay strong for the full 20-year verification period","Use a long-term signature format with renewable trusted timestamps, and plan for post-quantum signatures","Move to the largest elliptic curve keys available to raise the margin and shorten stored signatures","Record a hash of each signed permit in a public ledger to prove when each permit existed"],"a":[1],"e":"The problem is outliving certificates, CAs, and algorithms. Long-term signature formats embed validation data and timestamps so signatures stay verifiable after certificates expire, and renewing archive timestamps lets the agency move to stronger algorithms, including post-quantum ones, over time. Bigger RSA or elliptic curve keys don't handle certificate expiry or CA changes, and both fall to quantum attacks. A ledger proves existence, not who signed."},
{"id":"n101","d":3,"q":"Administrators reach servers through shared Windows jump servers. An attacker compromised one jump server and harvested the credentials of every admin who logged in over the following month. MFA is already required to sign in to the jump servers. The team has budget for a PAM platform. Which redesign BEST prevents this?","o":["Use PAM to inject credentials into brokered sessions, with JIT privilege, so admins do not handle them","Add a second MFA prompt on the jump servers for sessions that use domain admin group membership rights","Give each admin team its own jump server so a compromise affects fewer credentials at a time","Install EDR on the jump servers with alerts for credential dumping tools such as Mimikatz"],"a":[0],"e":"With credential injection, reusable admin passwords are not typed on or cached by the jump host, and JIT removes standing privilege, so a compromised host yields little. More MFA does not stop harvesting after sign-in. Per-team jump servers shrink the blast radius but keep the same weakness. EDR alerts may catch tools but not every technique."},
{"id":"n102","d":3,"q":"The team building an internal wiki rated low sensitivity proposes applying the full payment system control set, including HSMs and quarterly pen tests. During review, an engineer mentions that people sometimes paste network diagrams and service account passwords into wiki pages. The budget for the wiki is small. What should the architect recommend FIRST?","o":["Apply the payment system controls as proposed, since the wiki may hold secrets and sensitive network diagrams","Scan existing wiki content for secrets and sensitive material to confirm the classification is correct","Apply the low sensitivity baseline now, since the wiki was formally classified as low by its owner","Move the wiki to a SaaS platform so that the provider is responsible for most of the security controls"],"a":[1],"e":"Controls should match the data, and the engineer's comment suggests the classification may be wrong. Scanning content confirms what the wiki actually holds before choosing controls. Payment-level controls are excessive without that evidence. Applying the low baseline ignores the new information. Moving to SaaS does not change what data it holds."},
{"id":"n103","d":3,"q":"A firm uses cloud burst computing for genomic analysis. Contracts require that patient data processed there not be recoverable after jobs end. Jobs create volumes, snapshots, and temporary object storage, and the provider will not issue per-instance wipe certificates. Instances run on shared hardware. Which control BEST addresses data remanence?","o":["Overwrite each volume with random data before terminating instances at the end of each job run","Encrypt all job storage with a per-job customer-managed key, then destroy the key when the job ends","Run the jobs on dedicated hosts so no other tenant shares the hardware during or after processing","Request a data destruction attestation from the provider for each completed job batch on the contract"],"a":[1],"e":"Crypto-erase covers every place the data landed, including snapshots and replicas, by destroying the only key that can read it. Overwriting misses snapshots and may not reach all physical blocks. Dedicated hosts address co-tenancy, not remnant data. The provider will not issue per-job wipe evidence."},
{"id":"n104","d":3,"q":"A bank's mobile app team is worried about man-in-the-middle attacks from rogue CA certificates installed on users' devices through malicious profiles. The app runs on iOS and Android, and the bank changes its TLS certificates every year. Last year another bank's app was bricked for a week when its pinned certificate expired. Which control is MOST effective?","o":["Pin the bank's issuing CA or server public keys, with a backup pin and a safe pin update path","Enable HSTS on the API domain with a long max-age and submit the domain to the browser preload list","Check Certificate Transparency logs in the app before trusting a server certificate it receives","Rely on the platform default that excludes user-installed CA certificates from trust in third-party apps"],"a":[0],"e":"Public key pinning rejects certificates from any other CA, including rogue ones, and pinning keys with a backup avoids the bricking that comes from pinning a single expiring certificate. HSTS affects browsers, not app connections. CT checks are not enforced by default in apps and a rogue local CA's certificate may not be logged. Platform defaults differ, and iOS users can fully trust an installed profile CA."},
{"id":"n105","d":3,"q":"A company is redesigning guest Wi-Fi across 80 small offices that share access points with corporate networks and have no separate guest circuit. Legal wants guest sessions attributable if abuse complaints arrive, visitors complain about typing a shared password, and the network team wants to avoid adding new segments across the WAN. Which design is MOST appropriate?","o":["A guest network with a shared passphrase printed on posters and rotated monthly by each office manager","An open guest network on the corporate segment, with filters at the core switch that block traffic to all internal destinations","An encrypted open guest network with client isolation, a logged sponsor sign-in, and bandwidth and time limits","An open guest network with no sign-in page, relying on per-client bandwidth limits and DNS filtering only"],"a":[2],"e":"This option meets all three needs. Password-free encryption protects guests from each other over the air, sponsor sign-in makes sessions attributable for legal, and isolation keeps guests off corporate resources without new WAN segments. A shared passphrase annoys visitors and identifies no one. Putting guests on the corporate segment depends on filter correctness and breaks zone separation. No sign-in means no attribution."},
{"id":"p2","d":3,"q":"A retailer's Java monorepo has about 400 third-party dependencies. Legal wants license risk tracked and security wants known CVEs flagged. During discovery the architect finds that roughly a third of the libraries are vendored JAR files copied into lib/ folders years ago, often renamed, with no entry in any pom.xml. The team already runs a SAST tool tuned for Spring. Which capability matters MOST when selecting the new tool?","o":["Software composition analysis that identifies components by binary fingerprint or hash, not only by parsing build manifests","Software composition analysis with reachability analysis, so findings in dependencies the code doesn't call are deprioritized","SBOM generation from the Maven build at compile time, published as CycloneDX for each release of the monorepo","SAST with taint tracking that follows data from Spring controllers into library calls and flags unsafe sinks"],"a":[0],"e":"The deciding fact is the vendored, renamed JARs with no manifest entries. Only fingerprint or hash matching will find them. Reachability analysis is useful for triage but works on what the tool already found. A Maven-generated SBOM inherits the same manifest blind spot. SAST looks at first-party code flows and doesn't identify component versions or licenses."},
{"id":"p3","d":3,"q":"A medical device software vendor started shipping SBOMs after hospital customers demanded them. Now hospital security teams feed the SBOMs into scanners and open dozens of tickets per release for CVEs in bundled libraries. Engineering has confirmed most of those flagged functions are not compiled in or not reachable. Support is overwhelmed and two large customers threaten to pause deployments. The SBOMs are already signed and in CycloneDX format. What should the vendor add?","o":["VEX statements published with each release stating, per CVE, whether the product is affected and why","Richer SBOM fields, such as component hashes, supplier names, and dependency relationships","SLSA provenance attestations for each release showing which builder produced the artifacts and from which source commit","A public security advisory page listing each bundled library with its upgrade schedule and support end dates"],"a":[0],"e":"The problem is not identifying components but communicating exploitability. VEX is built for that: it lets the vendor say 'not affected, vulnerable code not present' per CVE so scanners can suppress noise. Richer SBOM fields improve matching but still produce the same CVE hits. Provenance proves build integrity, not exploitability. An advisory page helps humans but doesn't feed the customers' scanners."},
{"id":"p4","d":3,"q":"An attacker phished a developer and pushed a change to the .gitlab-ci.yml file on a new feature branch. The pipeline ran on that branch, pulled the production deploy token from CI variables, and pushed a backdoored image to production. The company has since enforced phishing-resistant MFA. The main branch already requires two approvals, and all commits must be signed through the company's keyless signing flow tied to SSO. Which control MOST directly closes the path used?","o":["Rotate all CI variables and move secrets into an external vault that pipelines query at runtime using their own short-lived job token","Bind production deploy credentials to protected branches and approval-gated environments so unreviewed branches can't get them","Add a policy scanner that checks CI configuration files for suspicious commands and fails the pipeline when it finds them","Require signed commits on feature branches as well as on main, verified against each developer's hardware-backed signing key"],"a":[1],"e":"The attacker's pipeline ran from an unreviewed branch yet reached production credentials. Tying those credentials to protected branches and gated environments removes that path. Moving secrets to a vault is good hygiene, but a branch pipeline could still query it with its job token. Config scanning is easy to evade. Signing wouldn't help because the attacker controlled the developer's SSO identity, which the keyless flow trusts."},
{"id":"p9","d":3,"q":"A developer accidentally made an internal repository public for about six hours. A scan shows the Git history contains a cloud access key for the billing account, a production database password, and a third-party payments API key. The database password is shared by four legacy services, and changing it needs a coordinated restart. The CISO's assistant is asking for a status update for the board. What should the team do FIRST?","o":["Rewrite Git history with a filter tool, force-push, and ask the hosting provider to purge cached views","Revoke and rotate the exposed credentials, starting with the cloud key and the payments key","Enable push protection and pre-commit secret scanning across the organization so this doesn't recur","Pull the cloud and payment provider logs for the six-hour window to see whether the keys were used"],"a":[1],"e":"A secret that was public must be treated as compromised, and automated scraping of public repos happens within minutes. Revoking and rotating comes first. History rewriting doesn't recall copies already taken. Prevention tooling matters but comes after containment. Log review is important and runs right after or in parallel, but waiting on it delays revocation of keys that may be in use."},
{"id":"p11","d":3,"q":"An insurer's claims portal lets customers upload PDFs, Word files, and photos. A back-end worker converts each upload into a preview image using an office document converter and an image library, then stores the original in object storage. The worker runs on the same VM pool as the claims API and has its database credentials. Upload checks already verify file type by magic bytes, cap size at 20 MB, and run antivirus. What should the architect prioritize?","o":["Add a second antivirus engine and a content disarm and reconstruction step before files reach the worker","Serve stored files from a separate domain with Content-Disposition: attachment and a restrictive content type","Run the converter in an isolated, ephemeral sandbox with no egress and no database credentials","Tighten the file type allowlist to PDF and JPEG only, rejecting Office formats at upload"],"a":[2],"e":"The riskiest component is the converter: complex parsers handling hostile input, running next to the API with database credentials. Isolating it limits the blast radius of a parser exploit. Extra scanning and CDR help but don't contain an exploit that gets through. Separate-domain serving protects users, not the back end. Dropping Office formats may break a business requirement and still leaves image and PDF parsers exposed."},
{"id":"p12","d":3,"q":"A critical remote code execution flaw is announced in a popular logging module. The vendor patch is expected in ten days. The company has about 60 Java services across three business units; some are internet-facing behind a CDN with a managed WAF and others are internal. The module is often pulled in as a transitive dependency, and the AppSec team doesn't know which services include it. SCA runs in CI but its results go to each team separately. What should happen FIRST?","o":["Query existing SCA and SBOM data across repos and running images to find affected services","Deploy a WAF virtual patch in blocking mode on the CDN for all internet-facing hostnames","Enable RASP agents on internet-facing Java services to block exploitation at runtime until the patch arrives","Ask each business unit to confirm within 48 hours whether their services use the logging module"],"a":[0],"e":"You can't prioritize or shield what you can't find, and transitive use means teams often don't know. Centralizing existing SCA and SBOM data scopes exposure fastest and most reliably. A WAF virtual patch is a strong next step for the exposed services but is blind to internal ones. RASP rollout takes time and needs scoping too. Asking teams relies on the knowledge they lack."},
{"id":"p15","d":3,"q":"An online retailer's checkout page loads six third-party scripts. To meet PCI DSS v4 script requirements, the team must authorize each script, assure its integrity, and detect unauthorized changes. One analytics vendor serves its script from a CDN, updates it without notice every few weeks, and has confirmed in the contract that customers may self-host a fixed version. Product only needs the vendor's basic page tracking. What is the BEST approach for this script?","o":["Add a Subresource Integrity hash to the vendor's CDN URL and update the hash whenever the script changes","Allow the vendor's CDN domain in the page's Content Security Policy and block other script sources","Self-host a reviewed, pinned copy with an SRI hash and update it only through change control","Load the script in a sandboxed iframe on the checkout page and monitor its network calls"],"a":[2],"e":"Since the contract allows self-hosting and only basic tracking is needed, a reviewed pinned copy gives authorization and integrity, with changes going through review. SRI on the vendor URL breaks every time the vendor silently updates. A CSP domain allowlist doesn't detect content changes on that domain. A sandboxed iframe may break the analytics and is more work than self-hosting a static copy."},
{"id":"p21","d":3,"q":"A company moved to Terraform and Amazon EKS last year. Developers run terraform apply and kubectl from their laptops with long-lived admin credentials. A SOC 2 Type II audit window opens in four months, and auditors will look for change control evidence. The platform team already scans Terraform in pull requests, but nothing stops a developer from applying unmerged code. Which change BEST addresses the core risk?","o":["Run policy-as-code checks in a pre-apply hook on developer laptops so noncompliant plans are blocked locally","Require MFA on the admin credentials and record terminal sessions when developers run apply against production","Make the pipeline the only identity with production write access, with a logged break-glass path for humans","Expand pull request scanning to include Kubernetes manifests and container images so issues are caught before merge"],"a":[2],"e":"The core risk is that production changes bypass review. Removing human write access and deploying only through the pipeline makes the reviewed path the only path and produces audit evidence. Local hooks can be skipped. MFA and session recording add accountability but still allow unreviewed changes. Broader PR scanning doesn't help when people can apply code that never went through a PR."},
{"id":"p27","d":3,"q":"A merchant is redesigning its web checkout and wants to minimize PCI DSS scope. The payment provider offers a full-page redirect, hosted card fields in iframes styled by the merchant, or a JavaScript library that collects card data in the merchant's own page and tokenizes it. The brand team insists the checkout stay on the merchant's domain with consistent styling. The site runs on a shared marketing CMS. Which option fits BEST?","o":["The full-page redirect to the provider, since card data stays off the merchant's own site","The provider's iframe-hosted card fields, styled to match the site and embedded on the merchant's page","The provider's JavaScript library that tokenizes card data in the merchant's own page before submission","Collect card data on the merchant's server and tokenize it immediately through the provider's API"],"a":[1],"e":"Hosted iframe fields keep card data in the provider's context, supporting the smallest SAQ scope, while meeting the brand requirement to stay on the merchant's domain. A redirect has similarly small scope but leaves the domain, which the brand team rejected. A JavaScript library running in the merchant's page puts the page in scope at a higher level. Server-side collection brings the merchant's systems fully into scope."},
{"id":"p32","d":3,"q":"A critical CVE is published for an open-source image library used by a public photo upload feature. The maintainers haven't responded in two weeks, and no patch exists. Analysis shows the flaw is in an optional TIFF decoder, and the product only needs JPEG and PNG. Replacing the library would take about two months of work. The release train is weekly. What should the architect recommend?","o":["Fork the library, write and test a fix for the decoder, and maintain the fork until the maintainers release a patch","Disable the TIFF decoder through configuration, reject TIFF uploads, and record a time-bound risk exception","Start the two-month effort to replace the library with an alternative that has an active maintainer base","Add a WAF rule that inspects uploaded files for the malformed TIFF headers described in the public advisory"],"a":[1],"e":"The vulnerable code path is optional and unneeded, so turning it off removes exposure quickly at low cost, and a dated exception keeps the residual risk visible. Forking creates a long-term maintenance burden for a decoder the product doesn't use. Replacement may be wise later but leaves two months of exposure. WAF inspection of binary uploads is fragile and easy to evade."},
{"id":"p36","d":3,"q":"A team runs about 300 serverless functions. Database credentials and API keys are set as plaintext environment variables in Terraform, which means they also appear in state files and in the console for anyone with read access to function configuration. Compliance now requires database credentials to rotate every 30 days without redeploying functions. Cold start latency is a known concern for the checkout functions. Which design is MOST appropriate?","o":["Encrypt the environment variables with a KMS key and decrypt them during function initialization","Grant each function's role only its own secrets in a secrets manager, fetched at init and cached briefly","Fetch each secret from the secrets manager on each invocation so functions see rotated values right away","Move the secrets into the function's container image at build time and rebuild the image when they rotate"],"a":[1],"e":"A secrets manager with per-function access supports automatic rotation without redeploys, and caching at init with a short TTL keeps cold starts and API calls low while picking up rotated values. KMS-encrypted environment variables still need a redeploy to rotate. Fetching on every invocation adds latency and cost and risks throttling. Baking secrets into images spreads them through registries and needs rebuilds."},
{"id":"p40","d":3,"q":"A team deploys code to production behind feature flags weeks before features launch. A reviewer finds that the flag is checked only in the React front end, so the new bulk-export API endpoints are live and callable by any authenticated user. Security testing of each feature is scheduled for the week before its flag is turned on. The flag service is a SaaS product the company already pays for. Which change should the architect require?","o":["Hide the new endpoints by giving them non-obvious paths until the feature is launched to customers","Keep the plan of testing each feature in the week before its flag is enabled, and add a sign-off gate","Enforce the flag server-side in the API, and security test dark-launched code before it deploys","Move the flag check from the React app into the API gateway's routing rules for the new paths"],"a":[2],"e":"Deployed code is attack surface whether a flag is on or not, so the flag must be enforced on the server and the code tested before it ships. Obscure paths are discoverable through the front-end bundle. Testing just before launch leaves weeks of exposure. Gateway routing rules help but separate the flag logic from the service and are easy to drift out of sync with the flag service."},
{"id":"p43","d":3,"q":"A company with about 500 services wants proof at deploy time that each artifact was built from reviewed source by the official CI system and not altered afterward. The build toolchains include several that aren't deterministic, and the platform team has one year and a small budget. Releases are already signed by a release manager's key, and SBOMs are generated for each build. Which approach BEST meets the goal?","o":["Rely on the existing release signing, adding a check at deploy that the release manager's signature is valid","Generate SLSA provenance on a hardened build platform and verify it, with the source commit, at deploy admission","Adopt reproducible builds and have an independent system rebuild each artifact and compare hashes before deploy","Attach the SBOM to each artifact and check at deploy that its component list matches the approved baseline"],"a":[1],"e":"Signed provenance from a hardened builder records what source and build produced each artifact, and admission checks enforce it at deploy. That fits 500 services in a year. Release signing shows who approved, not how or from what it was built. Reproducible builds are stronger but impractical with nondeterministic toolchains on this timeline. An SBOM lists contents but doesn't prove build origin."},
{"id":"p44","d":3,"q":"A tester bypassed a React app's input checks with an intercepting proxy and submitted oversized and malformed values that crashed a back-end job. The same API also serves a mobile app and a partner integration. The front-end team proposes signing each request in JavaScript so tampered requests are rejected. The mobile team already uses certificate pinning. What should the architect require?","o":["Adopt request signing in the SPA with a key bundled in the build, rotating it with each front-end release","Enforce the same validation server-side at the API boundary using shared schemas, for all clients alike","Extend certificate pinning to the web client through a service worker so proxies can't intercept traffic","Add WAF rules that enforce maximum lengths and allowed characters on the affected API parameters"],"a":[1],"e":"Anything running on the client can be bypassed, and the API has three different clients. Server-side validation from shared schemas protects all of them. A signing key shipped in JavaScript can be extracted. Pinning can be bypassed on a device the attacker controls and browsers don't support it this way. WAF rules are a coarse backstop and drift from the real data rules."},
{"id":"p48","d":3,"q":"A company's internal npm packages use unscoped names like billing-utils. The build agents are configured with both the internal registry and public npm. An attacker published billing-utils with a higher version number on public npm, and it was pulled in when a developer added a new internal dependency and regenerated the lockfile. The company already runs SCA. Which control BEST prevents a repeat?","o":["Commit lockfiles with integrity hashes and use npm ci in builds so resolved versions are fixed","Add an SCA policy that flags packages with known malicious indicators before they are installed","Move internal packages to a scope resolved only from the internal registry, and claim it on npm","Block public packages published in the last 30 days from being installed by the build agents"],"a":[2],"e":"The root cause is ambiguous resolution between registries. A scope bound to the internal registry, and claimed on the public one, removes that ambiguity. Lockfiles help but the attack happened when a new lockfile was generated. SCA malicious-package feeds lag new uploads. An age cooldown helps against fresh typosquats but an attacker can publish early and wait."},
{"id":"p50","d":3,"q":"A company's container images are built on a full Ubuntu base with compilers and package managers left in. The image scanner reports around 400 CVEs per image, and teams have stopped looking. The platform team has capacity for one initiative this quarter. Production clusters already enforce read-only root filesystems for about half the workloads. Which initiative MOST reduces vulnerability exposure?","o":["Patch critical and high CVEs monthly across all images, prioritizing by CVSS and internet exposure","Move to minimal or distroless bases via multi-stage builds, with centrally automated rebuilds","Enforce non-root users and read-only root filesystems for all workloads via admission","Deploy a runtime security agent that detects shell spawns and unexpected processes inside containers"],"a":[1],"e":"Most of the CVEs come from packages the app doesn't need. Minimal bases with multi-stage builds remove them and shrink the attack surface, and central rebuilds keep it current. Monthly patching treats the symptoms of a bloated base. Non-root and read-only are valuable hardening but don't reduce the vulnerable packages. Runtime detection finds exploitation after it starts."},
{"id":"p54","d":3,"q":"A health app shows lab results and stores downloaded PDFs on the device. A privacy review found copies of the PDFs in users' cloud device backups, which are outside the company's control. The team has already added certificate pinning and disabled screenshots on the results screen. The app runs on iOS and Android. Which control BEST addresses the finding?","o":["Hide app content in the task switcher snapshot so results aren't visible when the app is in the background","Exclude the PDFs from device backups and keep them in protected storage tied to the device's lock state","Disable copy and paste on screens that display lab values so users can't move data into other apps","Encrypt the PDFs with a key stored in shared preferences so backup copies are harder to read"],"a":[1],"e":"The finding is about backups, so excluding the files from backup and using platform data protection addresses it directly. Hiding snapshots and blocking the clipboard deal with other leakage paths. A key in shared preferences is likely backed up with the files, so the encryption adds little protection."},
{"id":"p62","d":3,"q":"A company wants to ensure that only approved executables and scripts run on its production Windows and Linux servers. EDR is deployed everywhere, servers are built from golden images, and file integrity monitoring alerts on changes to system directories. A red team still ran its own tools from a temp directory without being stopped. Which control BEST closes this gap at the host level?","o":["Tune EDR rules to alert on unsigned binaries run from temp directories","Application allowlisting through code integrity policies, such as WDAC and fapolicyd","Rebuild servers from golden images weekly so dropped tools are removed on a regular schedule","Extend file integrity monitoring to temp directories and alert on new executable files there"],"a":[1],"e":"Allowlisting enforces which code may run, so unapproved tools are blocked rather than reported. EDR and FIM are detective and the red team ran under them. Weekly rebuilds clear tools after the fact but don't stop them running in between."},
{"id":"o1","d":3,"q":"A national retailer's SIEM is licensed by daily ingest and now takes in 4 TB per day. About 70% of that volume is DNS, web proxy, and NetFlow data that analysts touch mainly during investigations; most detections run on EDR, identity, and firewall deny events. PCI DSS requires one year of audit log retention with the last three months immediately available. The SIEM vendor is shipping a new analyst UI next quarter. The CFO wants a 30% cost cut this fiscal year. Which design BEST meets the goal?","o":["Add a pipeline that filters DNS, proxy, and flow data into the SIEM and sends full copies to a searchable low-cost data lake","Move to a workload-based SIEM license so cost follows query compute instead of ingest volume, keeping all current sources in the hot tier","Stop sending raw DNS and proxy data and rely on the EDR's endpoint network telemetry for those investigations","Keep 90 days of all sources in the SIEM and roll older data to offline tape to meet the one-year rule"],"a":[0],"e":"A pipeline that routes bulky investigative data to a cheap, still-searchable tier cuts ingest cost while keeping detection data hot and meeting PCI retention. A license model change may help but leaves the 4 TB hot and is a procurement bet, not a design. Dropping DNS and proxy loses visibility for unmanaged devices that EDR does not see. The 90-day plus tape option meets PCI but barely touches ingest volume, which is the cost driver."},
{"id":"o3","d":3,"q":"An eight-person SOC spends about 40% of its time on phishing triage, copying indicators between the email gateway, the EDR console, the IdP, and the SIEM. The EDR vendor is pitching its XDR bundle, but the email gateway is from a different vendor with a three-year contract. The company also plans to replace its SIEM in about 18 months. Analysts work from shared runbooks in a wiki. Which approach BEST reduces the triage workload over the next few years?","o":["Adopt the EDR vendor's XDR platform and connect the third-party email gateway through its partner integrations","Accelerate the SIEM replacement and pick a platform with built-in phishing analytics and case management","Deploy a vendor-neutral SOAR platform and build an automated phishing enrichment and response playbook as the first use case","Hire two more analysts and convert the wiki runbooks into a structured, checklist-based triage procedure"],"a":[2],"e":"A standalone SOAR tool orchestrates across vendors and survives the planned SIEM swap, and phishing is a high-volume, repeatable use case ideal for automation. XDR from the EDR vendor tends to work best within its own stack, and the email gateway is locked in for three years. Pulling the SIEM replacement forward is a large, risky project to solve a workflow problem. More staff and better checklists help but do not remove the manual copying."},
{"id":"o4","d":3,"q":"A SOAR playbook disables any account flagged for impossible travel. Last month a network team changed VPN egress IPs, and the playbook disabled the CEO and 300 field sales staff during quarter-end. Sales leadership wants all automated containment turned off. The SOC says manual handling would add hours to response for real account takeovers. The SOAR platform runs on-premises and is due for a version upgrade. What is the BEST redesign?","o":["Add the new VPN egress ranges to the playbook's trusted locations and require network change tickets to notify the SOC","Pause automated containment until the rule logic is rewritten and tested, handling the travel anomaly alerts manually","Require the user's manager to approve each automated disable through a chat prompt before the playbook runs","Tier actions by confidence and impact: auto-revoke sessions and force MFA here, and have analysts approve disables"],"a":[3],"e":"Matching the action to confidence and blast radius keeps fast automated response while reserving disruptive steps for human approval. Allowlisting the egress IPs fixes this trigger but not the design flaw. Pausing automation gives up the speed benefit the SOC needs. Manager approval puts a non-security person in the loop and stalls response at night and on weekends."},
{"id":"o6","d":3,"q":"Payroll has a maximum tolerable downtime of 12 hours. IT can restore the payroll platform in 8 hours. After restore, payroll staff need about 5 hours to re-enter and reconcile transactions lost since the last recovery point. A vendor offers a premium tier that cuts restore to 6 hours for a higher annual fee. Payroll runs every two weeks. Which statement is MOST accurate?","o":["The plan is acceptable, since the 8-hour restore is well inside the 12-hour MTD","The MTD should be revised to 13 hours to match the tested recovery capability","The plan overshoots MTD by an hour; a faster restore or less rework would fix it","Work recovery time is a business activity and is planned separately from IT recovery"],"a":[2],"e":"RTO plus work recovery time must fit within MTD. Here 8 plus 5 is 13 hours, one over the limit. Either the premium restore tier or a tighter RPO that reduces rework closes the gap. RTO alone being under MTD is not enough. MTD comes from business impact and should not be stretched to fit IT capability. WRT is planned with the business but still counts against MTD."},
{"id":"o7","d":3,"q":"A payments platform needs an RTO of 15 minutes and near-zero data loss. The regulator requires a recovery site at least 150 miles from the primary. The database is licensed per core, and the budget allows one extra full-size copy plus a small one. The application is latency sensitive, with sub-5 ms write commit targets. Staff are split across two offices in the same metro. Which design BEST meets these requirements?","o":["Synchronous replication to a nearby metro site for zero loss, plus asynchronous replication to a distant warm standby site","Synchronous replication straight to a hot site 150 miles away so that each committed write exists in both places","Active-active across two distant regions with asynchronous replication and application-level conflict resolution","A cloud pilot-light environment in a distant region with continuous asynchronous replication and automated scale-up"],"a":[0],"e":"Synchronous replication over 150 miles adds round-trip latency that breaks the 5 ms commit target, so a metro synchronous copy gives near-zero RPO and fast failover while an async distant copy meets the regulator. Active-active across distant regions costs more cores than budgeted and risks conflicts. Pilot light usually cannot meet a 15-minute RTO and leaves some data loss."},
{"id":"o9","d":3,"q":"A retailer runs stores and e-commerce around the clock and has budget for six SOC analysts. A union agreement limits how many night shifts each analyst can work. The environment includes a custom point-of-sale platform whose logs an MDR provider cannot parse without custom work. The CISO wants after-hours coverage within six months. The SOC recently moved to a new office. Which operating model is MOST appropriate?","o":["Staff a 24x7 internal rotation using the six analysts on 12-hour shifts, with on-call backup for nights","Outsource all monitoring to an MSSP, including custom parsing of the point-of-sale logs","Use MDR for after-hours triage of standard telemetry, with the internal team owning POS detections","Run a follow-the-sun model with a partner SOC in another time zone handling nights"],"a":[2],"e":"Six analysts cannot sustainably cover 24x7, especially under night-shift limits, so a hybrid model gives coverage while keeping internal ownership of the custom POS detections the provider cannot handle. A six-person rotation burns out staff. Full outsourcing hands off detections the provider is weak at. A follow-the-sun partner is close to MDR but adds coordination cost without solving the POS gap."},
{"id":"o11","d":3,"q":"A company subscribes to 30 threat feeds that deliver millions of indicators, and analysts are swamped by matches on stale or low-value IOCs. The CTI team has two people. Leadership wants to add more feeds after a peer was breached. Business units have never been asked what intelligence they need. A threat intelligence platform is in the budget for next year. What should the architect do FIRST?","o":["Deploy the threat intelligence platform early to deduplicate, score, and expire indicators across feeds","Define intelligence requirements with stakeholders, then select and tune feeds to answer them","Automatically block high-confidence indicators at the firewall to cut alert volume","Reduce sources to government and ISAC feeds, which are typically better vetted than commercial ones"],"a":[1],"e":"Without defined requirements there is no basis for choosing feeds, scoring, or aging indicators, so requirements come first. A TIP helps manage volume but needs requirements to tune it. Auto-blocking cuts alerts but can block legitimate traffic and does not fix relevance. Cutting to ISAC feeds is a guess that may drop the intelligence that matters most."},
{"id":"o13","d":3,"q":"An attacker has a foothold on an engineering file server and is actively staging archives of export-controlled designs for outbound transfer. Federal agents involved in a broader investigation ask the company to keep monitoring quietly for a few more weeks. The legal team is concerned about export control violations. The server runs an older OS that is due for replacement. What should the incident lead do?","o":["Keep monitoring at the agents' request and record all activity to support the broader case","Isolate and rebuild the server immediately and reset all engineering credentials the same day","Block the exfil path and protect the data, keep watching other footholds, and get legal and agency sign-off","Move the designs to a new share and leave decoy files in place to track the attacker's next moves"],"a":[2],"e":"The deciding factor is ongoing harm against intelligence value. Active staging of export-controlled data is serious harm, so the exfil path must be stopped, while monitoring can continue where it does not risk the data, with legal and law enforcement coordination. Pure monitoring accepts the loss. An immediate rebuild may tip off the attacker before the full scope is known. Moving files and planting decoys is useful deception but leaves the exfil channel open."},
{"id":"o14","d":3,"q":"A Linux server in a colocation facility is suspected of running a kernel rootkit. The responder has remote shell access only, and the system owner wants to reboot it within the hour to restore service. Forensic analysis may support a lawsuit. The server also forwards syslog to a central SIEM, and the colocation provider can supply console access if needed. Which collection sequence is BEST?","o":["Run the server's own netstat and ps to record connections and processes, then capture memory, then image the disk","Capture memory with trusted tools from external media, then network state, then a disk image, then remote logs","Pull SIEM logs first to scope the incident, then capture memory and network state, then shut down and image the disk","Shut down cleanly to protect file system consistency, then image the disk with a write blocker, then pull logs"],"a":[1],"e":"Memory is the most volatile and holds rootkit artifacts, and it must be captured with trusted tools since a rootkit can hide from local binaries. Disk follows, and remote logs are least volatile and can wait. Running local netstat and ps relies on tools the rootkit may control and changes memory first. Starting with SIEM logs wastes the volatile window. A clean shutdown destroys memory evidence."},
{"id":"o15","d":3,"q":"A company suspects a departing engineer stole source code. An IT admin copied files from the engineer's laptop to a USB drive and emailed screenshots to HR. Counsel now says the evidence may be challenged in court. The company uses Microsoft 365 and a commercial EDR. Leadership asks what the forensic readiness program should focus on to avoid this outcome in future. What is MOST important?","o":["Automated legal hold in Microsoft 365 so that mailboxes and OneDrive content are preserved on demand","Documented procedures with chain of custody, hashing at acquisition, and trained staff using validated tools","A year of full EDR telemetry retention so that file access evidence exists whenever an employee departs","A data loss prevention policy that blocks source code uploads to personal cloud storage, email, and removable media"],"a":[1],"e":"Admissibility depends on showing evidence was collected properly and not altered: chain of custody, hashes taken at acquisition, and repeatable methods by trained people. Legal hold preserves data but does not make ad hoc collection defensible. Longer EDR retention gives more data but not defensible handling. DLP is prevention, not evidence handling."},
{"id":"o16","d":3,"q":"During an investigation, analysts could not line up events from cloud services, which log in UTC, with on-premises firewalls that log local time without a timezone field. The SIEM stamped those firewall events with ingest time, and some arrived hours late after a collector backlog. One domain controller had also drifted four minutes, which surfaced as Kerberos errors. The SOC recently upgraded its SIEM storage. Which design change BEST fixes the root problem?","o":["Configure the SIEM to use ingest time for all sources so each event follows one consistent clock","Point all devices at a public NTP pool and add per-source timezone offsets in the SIEM parsers","Use an authenticated internal NTP hierarchy, log in UTC with offsets, and keep both event and ingest time","Increase collector capacity to clear the backlog and alert when a domain controller drifts beyond one minute"],"a":[2],"e":"Reliable timelines need trusted, synchronized clocks, timestamps with explicit timezones, and both event time and ingest time so delays are visible. Ingest time hides delays like the backlog seen here. A public pool lacks authentication and parser offsets break on daylight saving changes. More collector capacity and drift alerts treat symptoms."},
{"id":"o17","d":3,"q":"A company's scanners report 200,000 open findings, 30,000 of them CVSS 9 or higher. Remediation teams work strictly by CVSS score and cannot keep up. Last quarter an attacker exploited a medium-severity CVE on an internet-facing VPN appliance that was on the CISA KEV list. The asset inventory has no business criticality or exposure tags. The scanning tool's license renews next month. What should the architect do FIRST?","o":["Set a 15-day SLA for all CVSS 9+ findings and report team compliance to the executive committee","Tag assets for exposure and criticality, then rank open findings by KEV listing, EPSS score, and those tags","Replace the scanner at renewal with a platform that includes built-in risk-based scoring","Move from monthly to weekly scanning so newly exploited vulnerabilities are found sooner"],"a":[1],"e":"Risk-based prioritization needs exploit likelihood (KEV, EPSS) plus asset exposure and criticality, and the inventory gap blocks that, so tagging comes first. A tighter CVSS SLA keeps the wrong ranking and would have missed the exploited medium CVE. A new tool still needs asset context. Faster scans add findings without better triage."},
{"id":"o18","d":3,"q":"A hospital's change policy requires 30 days of testing for every patch. A critical remote code execution flaw in its remote access gateway is being actively exploited, and the vendor has released both a patch and a configuration mitigation. Clinicians rely on the gateway for after-hours access to imaging systems. The change advisory board meets weekly, with the next meeting in six days. What should happen?","o":["Wait for the next CAB meeting so the patch can enter testing through the normal approval process","Take the gateway offline until the patch completes its 30-day testing cycle to remove all exposure","Use the emergency change path: apply the mitigation now, hunt for compromise, then fast-track the patch","Apply the vendor patch to production tonight, skipping testing, because the exposure is actively exploited"],"a":[2],"e":"An emergency path lets the hospital cut risk at once with the vendor mitigation, check whether it was already compromised, and patch on an accelerated test cycle. Waiting six days leaves an exploited flaw open. Taking the gateway offline for a month hurts clinical care. Patching untested in production may be justified in some cases, but here a safer mitigation exists and the hunt is missing."},
{"id":"o19","d":3,"q":"An outage was traced to an undocumented firewall rule a contractor added through a shared admin account. The network team has an ITSM tool, nightly configuration backups, and TACACS+ command accounting that nobody reviews. Management wants to catch unauthorized changes quickly without slowing approved work. The firewall pair is due for a hardware refresh next year. Which control BEST detects such changes?","o":["Require dual approval in the ITSM tool for firewall changes before engineers can start the work","Email nightly config diffs to the network team so they can spot unexpected changes","Detect config drift against the approved baseline and reconcile each change to a ticket","Review TACACS+ command logs each month and flag commands run from shared accounts"],"a":[2],"e":"Comparing actual configuration to a baseline and matching each change to an approved ticket catches unauthorized changes regardless of how they were made. Dual approval is a preventive control that this contractor bypassed. Emailed diffs show changes but rely on people to judge which are approved. Monthly log review is slow, and shared accounts hide who acted."},
{"id":"o20","d":3,"q":"In a recent incident, attackers with domain admin rights loaded a vulnerable signed driver to kill the EDR agent on 40 servers, then deployed ransomware. Tamper protection was already enabled. The EDR console showed the servers as offline for two hours, and analysts assumed it was patching. The servers run a mix of Windows Server 2019 and 2022. Which improvement MOST directly addresses this attack?","o":["Turn on the EDR product's tamper protection policy for all server groups and lock its settings","Alert when agents go silent unexpectedly, correlated with other telemetry, and block vulnerable drivers","Deploy a second EDR product on critical servers so that one agent can still cover if the other is disabled","Remove local admin rights from server operators and require just-in-time elevation through PAM"],"a":[1],"e":"The attack used a driver to bypass tamper protection, and the silence went unnoticed. Alerting on unexpected agent loss and blocking known vulnerable drivers both target this path. Tamper protection was already on. A second EDR adds cost and the same driver can kill both. Removing local admin is good hygiene but the attacker already had domain admin."},
{"id":"o21","d":3,"q":"A company has 120 cloud accounts in one organization. Account owners enabled API audit logging inconsistently, and logs sit in storage inside each account. Threat detection runs in only two accounts. A review found that an admin in any member account can stop logging and delete its log data. The SOC wants solid detection and investigation of control-plane activity. Most workloads run in two regions. What should be done FIRST?","o":["Turn on the provider's managed threat detection service across the whole organization, managed from a delegated admin account","Send every account's API audit logs, in all regions, to a locked-down archive account that member admins can't change","Turn on network flow logging in all accounts and forward it to the SIEM for analysis","Deploy a posture management tool to flag accounts where audit logging is off or set up incorrectly"],"a":[1],"e":"Detection and forensics depend on complete audit logs that a compromised account can't tamper with, so central, protected logging comes first. Managed threat detection is valuable but doesn't give the SOC a retained, protected record. Flow logs show network traffic, not API calls. Posture tools report the gap without fixing log integrity."},
{"id":"o23","d":3,"q":"A company's IR plan assumes the team will coordinate in Microsoft Teams and email. In a tabletop exercise the scenario included an attacker with Global Admin in Entra ID, and the team realized it could not trust any tool tied to that tenant. Some responders use corporate iPhones enrolled in Intune. Legal requires that incident communications be retained. What should the IR architecture include?","o":["A private Teams channel limited to IR members, with retention policies set by legal","A pre-built channel outside corporate identity, with offline contact lists and retention configured","Personal mobile numbers for all responders, kept in a shared contact sheet in SharePoint","Satellite phones for the IR lead and CISO so leadership can coordinate during an outage"],"a":[1],"e":"Out-of-band communication has to sit outside the compromised identity plane, be set up before an incident, and meet retention rules. A private Teams channel still depends on the compromised tenant. Personal numbers in SharePoint rely on the same tenant and lack retention. Satellite phones cover only two people and ignore records."},
{"id":"o24","d":3,"q":"A mid-size firm with a three-person SOC wants high-fidelity detection of credential theft and lateral movement in Active Directory. Its existing honeypot appliance fires daily, mostly from the internal vulnerability scanner and IT discovery tools. The team has little time for tuning. The firm also plans to move file shares to SharePoint next year. Which deception design gives the BEST signal with the least effort?","o":["Deploy several high-interaction honeypots on internal subnets to observe attacker behavior","Plant fake privileged credentials and decoy accounts on endpoints, alerting on their use","Build a honeynet in the DMZ to collect attacker tools and techniques for threat intel","Exclude the scanner and discovery tools on the current honeypot and keep it running"],"a":[1],"e":"Honeytoken credentials have no legitimate use, so any use is a strong signal of theft or lateral movement, and they need little upkeep. High-interaction honeypots need care and maintenance a small team lacks. A DMZ honeynet gathers external intel, not internal lateral movement. Tuning the current appliance helps but network decoys still draw noise from legitimate tools."},
{"id":"o25","d":3,"q":"A SOC reports alerts closed per analyst and tickets handled per day to the executive committee. After a breach that went undetected for weeks, the board asks for metrics that show whether the SOC limits attacker impact. The SOC uses an MDR provider at night and a ticketing system that timestamps every state change. Which pair of metrics BEST answers the board?","o":["Share of alerts closed within SLA, and analyst utilization across all shifts","Time to detect and time to contain, measured from first attacker action","Number of blocked threats per month, and phishing simulation click rate","Detection true-positive rate, and percentage of ATT&CK techniques covered"],"a":[1],"e":"Limiting impact depends on how fast the SOC finds and stops attackers, measured from when the attack actually started, not from when an alert fired. SLA closure and utilization measure throughput. Blocked threats and click rates measure prevention and awareness. True-positive rate and coverage matter for tuning but do not show impact."},
{"id":"o26","d":3,"q":"A manufacturer's SOC monitors only IT. The plant-floor OT network has no monitoring, and OT engineers worry that agents or active scans could trip safety systems or stop lines. Several HMIs run unsupported Windows versions certified by the machine vendor. Managed switches at the supervisory level support SPAN. The board wants OT threat visibility after a peer's ransomware reached its plants. What is the MOST appropriate approach?","o":["Install EDR agents on engineering workstations and HMIs during the next planned maintenance window","Run an active asset discovery scan during a shutdown to build the OT inventory before monitoring","Add passive ICS-aware network monitoring on SPAN ports feeding the SOC, with OT playbooks","Put a firewall between IT and OT first, then decide on monitoring after segmentation"],"a":[2],"e":"Passive ICS-aware monitoring gives visibility without touching fragile systems, uses the SPAN capability that exists, and joint playbooks bring OT engineers into response. EDR on vendor-certified HMIs can break support and stability. An active scan during shutdown is possible but does not give ongoing visibility. Segmentation is important but the board asked for visibility now."},
{"id":"o28","d":3,"q":"A company runs its own data center and is building a continuity plan for a pandemic where up to 40% of staff may be out for weeks. Some tasks need people on site, such as tape rotation and hardware swaps. Three senior engineers hold most of the knowledge about the storage platform. The VPN concentrator is already near capacity. The data center is co-located with a regional office. Which planning element is MOST important?","o":["Buy additional VPN licenses and bandwidth so that most staff can work remotely for long periods","Contract remote-hands services from a third party for on-site tasks such as tape and hardware work","Map critical roles and knowledge gaps, cross-train and document, and set minimum on-site staffing","Start moving workloads to cloud so that the dependence on on-site data center staff is reduced"],"a":[2],"e":"A people-focused disruption is mainly a staffing and knowledge problem, so mapping critical roles, removing single points of knowledge, and defining minimum on-site staffing comes first. More VPN capacity and remote hands are useful parts of the plan but do not fix the knowledge risk. Moving to cloud is a long-term strategy, not a continuity plan."},
{"id":"o29","d":3,"q":"A company needs Windows event logs and EDR telemetry from 30,000 laptops, about 70% of which are rarely on the corporate network. Today collection uses Windows Event Forwarding over the VPN, which uses split tunneling. Some users are in countries with metered bandwidth. The privacy office wants collection limited to what security needs. The laptops are refreshed every four years. Which design is MOST appropriate?","o":["Switch to a persistent full-tunnel VPN so that Windows Event Forwarding can reach all of the laptops","Collect only when laptops connect to the VPN, and accept the gaps for remote periods","Rely on EDR telemetry alone and stop collecting Windows event logs from laptops","Use agents that send filtered events to a cloud collector over mutual TLS, buffering offline"],"a":[3],"e":"Internet-facing collection with mutual TLS, local buffering, and agent-side filtering works on any network, limits bandwidth, and supports privacy. Full-tunnel VPN adds load and user impact just for logging. Collecting only on VPN leaves big blind spots. EDR alone misses some OS events investigators rely on."},
{"id":"o32","d":3,"q":"A SOC manager is proposing a threat hunting program to leadership. The team has strong EDR and SIEM telemetry, a small CTI function, and a backlog of low-priority alerts. Leadership asks which plan would add value beyond the existing automated detection. The SOC also plans to adopt a new case management tool next quarter, and two analysts have hunting training from a recent course. Which plan is BEST?","o":["Have analysts work through the lowest-priority alert queue each day to find threats that were missed","Sweep the environment weekly for indicators from new threat feeds and report any matches found","Run hypothesis-driven hunts based on intel and ATT&CK, and turn what they find into new detections","Run authenticated vulnerability scans of crown-jewel systems monthly and review the exposure"],"a":[2],"e":"Hunting is a proactive, hypothesis-driven search for activity that evaded detection, and its value compounds when findings become new automated detections. Working low-priority alerts is triage. IOC sweeps are a narrow form of hunting that finds only known indicators. Vulnerability scanning is exposure management, not hunting."},
{"id":"o33","d":3,"q":"A manufacturer has subsidiaries in the EU, China, and Brazil and a global SOC in the US. Chinese data rules restrict exporting certain personal and important data, and the EU subsidiary's works council has raised concerns about monitoring. The company can afford one SIEM vendor. The SOC wants to correlate attacks that span regions. Most attacks seen so far target the US business. Which approach is BEST?","o":["Centralize all logs in a US-hosted SIEM and use standard contractual clauses to cover the EU transfers","Run separate SIEMs and regional SOCs in each jurisdiction with no shared data between them","Pseudonymize all user fields before shipping logs to the US SIEM so that no personal data leaves","Keep logs in-region on one platform with federated search, sharing only allowed metadata centrally"],"a":[3],"e":"Regional storage on one platform keeps data where law requires while federated search and permitted metadata let the global SOC correlate. A US-only SIEM may cover EU transfers with SCCs but not Chinese export rules. Fully separate SOCs lose cross-region correlation and cost more. Pseudonymizing every field hurts investigations and may not satisfy rules on important data."},
{"id":"o34","d":3,"q":"After a major incident, an executive asks that the post-incident review name the administrator whose misconfiguration allowed initial access so HR can take action. Legal expects litigation and wants the review protected where possible. The IR firm has offered to write the full report. The company plans to run the review in its new collaboration tool. What should the architect recommend?","o":["Name the individual in the review so that accountability is clear and the same mistake is not repeated","Hold a blameless root cause review under counsel's direction and track each fix through to completion","Delay the review until the litigation is resolved so that nothing written can be used against the firm","Have the outside IR firm write the review alone so that internal politics do not shape the findings"],"a":[1],"e":"A blameless review focused on systemic causes gets honest input, and running it under counsel where appropriate helps with the litigation concern. Tracked actions make it lead to change. Naming one person discourages candor and misses system failures. Waiting for litigation loses lessons while memories fade. The IR firm can contribute but internal owners must accept and act on findings."},
{"id":"o35","d":3,"q":"A company's SIEM, SOAR, log collectors, and security jump hosts all run in the primary data center. The DR plan fails business apps over to a secondary site with a 4-hour RTO, but security tools are not in scope. In a ransomware tabletop, the team realized the SOC would be blind during the event it most needs to see. An MDR provider monitors nights using the on-premises collectors. Which design consideration is MOST important?","o":["Add the security tools to the DR runbook with the same 4-hour RTO as the business applications","Cluster the SIEM and SOAR inside the primary data center so a single server failure is tolerated","Rely on the MDR provider's own console for visibility while the primary data center is down","Host the monitoring and response tooling outside the failure domain that it is meant to watch"],"a":[3],"e":"Security tooling must stay up when the monitored environment fails, so it belongs outside that failure domain, for example in cloud or a separate site with independent identity. Adding it to DR still leaves the SOC blind for hours. Local clustering does not survive a site or ransomware event. The MDR depends on the same on-premises collectors."},
{"id":"o36","d":3,"q":"A leaked AWS access key belonging to the CI/CD pipeline was used to create two IAM users with access keys and a role that trusts an external AWS account. The pipeline deploys to production every hour, and the release manager warns that breaking it will delay a launch. CloudTrail is enabled organization-wide. The account also runs a legacy batch job nobody owns. Which action should be taken FIRST?","o":["Delete the attacker's IAM users and role right away to remove persistence, then investigate","Rotate the pipeline key, update the pipeline, and then review CloudTrail for other activity","Deactivate the leaked and attacker keys, block the new users and role, and revoke active sessions","Apply an SCP that denies all actions in the account until the investigation is complete"],"a":[2],"e":"Containment should cut off every attacker access path, including the external role trust, while keeping evidence intact, so deactivate and block rather than delete. Deleting destroys artifacts investigators need. Rotating only the pipeline key leaves the attacker's own users and role working. A deny-all SCP stops the attacker but also stops production, which is more disruption than needed when targeted actions work."},
{"id":"o37","d":3,"q":"A bug bounty researcher reports a SQL injection flaw in the customer portal, and the triage team confirms it. Development says a fix will ship in the next sprint, three weeks away. Code history shows the flaw has existed for 18 months. The portal sits behind a WAF in monitoring mode. The researcher has asked about a bonus for a fast report. What should the vulnerability handling process ensure NEXT?","o":["Notify all portal customers now that their account data may have been exposed through the flaw","Ask the researcher to retest after the sprint and pay the bounty once the fix is verified","Enforce a WAF rule now, search old logs for exploitation, and ship the fix within the SLA","Raise the fix to the top of the next sprint and pay the bounty based on the program terms"],"a":[2],"e":"A confirmed critical flaw needs quick risk reduction (a blocking WAF rule), a look back for past exploitation given 18 months of exposure, and a fix within the critical SLA. Notifying customers before knowing whether data was accessed is premature. Retest and payment are program steps, not risk handling. Reprioritizing the sprint leaves three weeks of exposure and skips the exploitation check."},
{"id":"o39","d":3,"q":"A trading firm's application audit logs may be used in litigation. They are written locally on Linux servers where several admins have root. The SIEM receives a copy, but SIEM admins belong to the same team and can delete indexes. Counsel wants the logs to stand up to tampering claims. The firm is also moving to a new SIEM vendor next year. Which control is MOST effective?","o":["Restrict root on the servers through PAM with session recording and approval workflows for sudo","Tighten SIEM role-based access so that only the SIEM team lead can delete indexed log data","Hash the log files daily and store the hashes in a separate directory on the same server","Forward logs in near real time to a WORM store in a separate account with hash checks"],"a":[3],"e":"Shipping logs quickly to write-once storage outside the admins' control, with hashing or chaining, makes tampering detectable and hard. PAM on root helps but local logs remain editable. SIEM RBAC still leaves one admin able to delete. Hashes kept on the same server can be changed by the same root user."},
{"id":"o40","d":3,"q":"Analysts receive about 5,000 alerts per day, and 95% are false positives. Some analysts have started bulk-closing entire rules. The SOC manager wants to buy an ML-based alert triage product, and a vendor has offered a free pilot. Detection rules have no owners or performance tracking. The SIEM was upgraded last quarter, and its license allows unlimited rules. What is the BEST approach?","o":["Pilot the ML triage product to rank alerts and auto-close those it scores as likely benign","Hire more tier-1 analysts to work the queue down while the detection rules are reviewed over time","Raise severity thresholds so analysts see only high and critical alerts on their dashboards","Track precision per rule, tune or retire weak rules, correlate alerts, and add asset context"],"a":[3],"e":"The root cause is unmanaged detection content. Measuring each rule, tuning or retiring weak ones, and correlating and enriching alerts fixes the source. ML triage can help later but hides bad rules and needs good inputs. More staff absorbs noise without reducing it. Higher thresholds hide true positives that sit in medium severity."},
{"id":"o42","d":3,"q":"A Linux VM in an Azure scale set shows signs of compromise. The scale set may remove the instance during the next scale-in, which usually happens overnight. The app team wants to delete it and redeploy from a clean image. Legal says the evidence may be needed. The workload processes customer orders and has two other healthy instances. Which approach preserves evidence BEST?","o":["Delete and redeploy from a clean image, keeping the platform logs as the evidence record","Stop the VM to freeze its current state, then snapshot the OS and data disks and hash the snapshots","Protect it from scale-in, capture memory, snapshot disks, isolate it with an NSG, and log custody","Copy the application and system logs to a storage account, then let the scale set remove it"],"a":[2],"e":"Scale-in protection stops the platform from destroying the evidence, memory is captured before anything else, disks are snapshotted, and network isolation contains the attacker while keeping the VM running. Deleting or letting it scale in loses disk and memory. Stopping the VM loses memory before it is captured."},
{"id":"o43","d":3,"q":"A company rates incident severity by the CVSS score of the exploited vulnerability and the number of affected hosts. Last month a single compromised server in payroll was rated low and sat untouched for three days, while a noisy adware outbreak on 200 kiosks got top priority. The company is regulated and holds employee banking data. The SOC also wants to automate severity scoring. Which criteria should drive severity?","o":["Number of affected hosts and total alert volume, weighted by the analyst's confidence","CVSS of the exploited flaw, adjusted up when public exploit code is available","Business impact, data and regulatory exposure, and the attack's scope and stage","Asset count and the time since detection, so older incidents rise automatically"],"a":[2],"e":"Severity should reflect harm to the business: which systems and data are affected, regulatory exposure, and how far the attack has progressed. Host count and alert volume rewarded the noisy adware. CVSS rates a vulnerability, not an incident. Aging helps stalled cases but does not rank impact."},
{"id":"o46","d":3,"q":"Ransomware encrypted a hospital's EHR database, and clinicians have been on paper records for three days. Immutable backups exist at 1, 7, and 30 days old. The investigation so far found persistence through a GPO-deployed scheduled task and a compromised backup admin account, but initial access is not yet dated. The EHR vendor is on site, and the board is pressing for service by tomorrow. Which recovery approach is BEST?","o":["Restore the 30-day backup, since it is the one most likely to predate the attacker and carry no persistence","Restore into an isolated environment, remove known persistence, reset credentials, reconnect in stages","Restore the 1-day backup to production now and put enhanced EDR monitoring on the restored servers","Rebuild the EHR servers from scratch and import the latest data exports provided by the EHR vendor"],"a":[1],"e":"With access not dated, any backup could carry persistence, so restoring into isolation, checking for the known mechanisms, and resetting compromised credentials before staged reconnection limits reinfection while still moving fast. The 30-day backup loses a month of clinical data and may still be infected. Restoring straight to production risks immediate reinfection through the GPO and backup account. A full rebuild is slower than the clinical need allows."},
{"id":"o48","d":3,"q":"A company uses Box as its corporate file platform. Many employees also have personal Box and Google Drive accounts. TLS inspection is enabled on the secure web gateway, except for a few banking and health sites. The company has a CASB that can run inline or through APIs. Leadership wants to detect and stop data leaving through personal cloud storage without breaking corporate Box. Which combination is MOST effective?","o":["Block the Box and Google Drive domains at the gateway and allow exceptions on request","Use API-mode CASB to scan the corporate Box tenant for sensitive files shared externally","Use inline tenant restrictions to allow only corporate instances, plus DLP and upload volume alerts","Use endpoint DLP to block uploads of confidential-labeled files to web storage from managed laptops, with alerts"],"a":[2],"e":"Inline instance awareness tells corporate from personal tenants on the same domain, so personal uploads can be blocked while corporate Box works, and DLP plus volume alerts catch misuse. Blocking the domains breaks corporate Box. API-mode CASB sees only the corporate tenant. Endpoint DLP depends on labeling and misses unlabeled data."},
{"id":"o50","d":3,"q":"A company's CMDB is about 60% accurate. Vulnerability scans keep missing servers, and an acquisition last year added subnets nobody has fully mapped. EDR covers most laptops but few servers. Cloud accounts are created by business units. Last month an unknown, unpatched server was used for initial access. The CMDB vendor just released a new UI. Which approach BEST improves the asset inventory for security operations?","o":["Make CMDB updates mandatory in change management and audit compliance quarterly","Run a full network discovery scan each quarter, including the acquired subnets","Continuously reconcile CMDB, EDR, cloud, network scan, and IdP data","Require new assets to register through NAC before they receive an IP address"],"a":[2],"e":"No single source is complete, so continuously combining CMDB, EDR, cloud, network, and identity data finds unknown assets and those missing controls. Mandatory CMDB updates help but rely on people. Quarterly scans leave long gaps and miss cloud. NAC registration helps on campus networks but not cloud or existing gaps."},
{"id":"o51","d":3,"q":"The SOC sees a Cobalt Strike beacon on a sales rep's laptop. The rep is working from a customer site on guest Wi-Fi, and the company does not use always-on VPN. The laptop runs a cloud-managed EDR and is enrolled in Intune. The rep is about to join a deal meeting and has not answered calls. The laptop's last full scan was two days ago. Which action is MOST effective right now?","o":["Disable the rep's account in the IdP and revoke sessions so the attacker cannot use the identity","Use EDR network containment to cut all traffic but the EDR cloud, then investigate remotely","Push a restrictive firewall profile through Intune and wait for the device to check in and apply","Keep calling the rep and ask them to disconnect from the Wi-Fi and power down the laptop for pickup"],"a":[1],"e":"EDR containment works on any network and leaves the investigation channel open, so it stops the beacon immediately. Disabling the account helps but the beacon still runs on the device. Intune policy may take time to apply. Calling the rep is slow and powering down loses memory evidence."},
{"id":"o53","d":3,"q":"A company already runs a SIEM with 15 log sources and three years of retention for compliance. Its EDR vendor is pitching XDR and says the SIEM could be retired. XDR would cover endpoint, identity, and email from that vendor plus a few third-party connectors. The SOC is small and struggles to pivot between consoles. Which statement BEST describes XDR's value here?","o":["It can retire the SIEM, since XDR ingests the same log types and meets compliance retention","Its main value is lower licensing cost through consolidating several tools with one vendor","Correlated detection and response across its integrated controls, alongside the SIEM","Its value is mostly better endpoint detection than a standalone EDR gives"],"a":[2],"e":"XDR's main benefit is correlating signals across endpoint, identity, and email and acting from one place, which helps a small SOC. It usually does not match a SIEM's broad third-party ingestion and long retention, so the SIEM likely stays for compliance. Cost savings may happen but are not the primary benefit. The endpoint engine is often the same as the EDR."},
{"id":"o58","d":3,"q":"Active Directory is fully compromised. IR shows initial access about 60 days ago, with domain admin rights soon after. DC backups go back 180 days. The DCs are virtualized, and the hypervisor admin account was also compromised. The company runs 400 apps that rely on AD. Leadership wants a plan that restores trust without an 18-month migration. Which approach is MOST appropriate?","o":["Reset all passwords and the krbtgt account twice in place, then monitor closely for attacker activity","Restore the newest DC backup to production and remove the persistence that IR has identified","Build a new forest and migrate users and applications to it over the coming year and a half","Run a documented forest recovery from a pre-compromise backup on clean, isolated infrastructure"],"a":[3],"e":"A forest recovery from a backup taken before the compromise, built on hypervisors the attacker never touched, restores trust with far less disruption than a new forest. In-place resets leave unknown persistence. The newest backup carries the attacker's changes. A new forest is valid but is the long migration leadership wants to avoid."},
{"id":"o60","d":3,"q":"A company runs production in one cloud region across three availability zones. Backups use the provider's native service and are stored in the same account and region. The backup service supports cross-region and cross-account copies. The CFO wants to limit added cost. The company also uses a separate SaaS tool for endpoint backup. Which change addresses the MOST significant risk?","o":["Spread the backup vaults across all three availability zones in the current region","Copy backups to another region in a separate account with immutable locks","Turn on object versioning for backup storage so deleted copies can be restored","Increase backup frequency from daily to hourly to reduce potential data loss"],"a":[1],"e":"With production and backups in one account and region, a regional outage or account compromise can take out both. Cross-region, cross-account, immutable copies address both threats. Spreading across zones does not help with regional or account events. Versioning in the same account can be undone by an admin attacker. More frequent backups improve RPO but not survivability."},
{"id":"o61","d":3,"q":"About 200 development teams each own their AWS accounts. The cloud workload protection platform sends roughly 3,000 alerts a week, all to the SOC, which lacks the context to judge most of them. Tickets the SOC forwards to dev teams often sit for weeks. Most alerts are posture findings, but a few are runtime threats like reverse shells. The platform also generates SBOM reports. Which operating model is MOST effective?","o":["Keep all alerts in the SOC and add two cloud specialists to handle the volume with better context","Send all alerts directly to the owning dev teams, who know their workloads and can fix issues quickly","Route posture findings to owners with SLAs; the SOC keeps runtime threats and escalates misses","Suppress posture findings in the SOC queue and alert only on runtime threats to reduce noise for now"],"a":[2],"e":"Owners are best placed to fix posture issues, while runtime threats need SOC response. Routing by type, with SLAs and SOC escalation, gives both speed and accountability. Keeping everything in the SOC does not scale. Sending runtime threats to dev teams delays incident response. Suppressing posture findings drops real risk."},
{"id":"o62","d":3,"q":"Analysts confirm an attacker exploited a zero-day in the company's VPN appliance. No patch exists yet, but the vendor has published a mitigation and an integrity check tool. About 8,000 remote staff depend on the VPN daily. The appliance also terminates a few site-to-site tunnels. Security leadership is debating options. What should the response architecture support?","o":["Shut the VPN down until a patch ships, and move remote staff to a virtual desktop service","Factory reset the appliance and restore its saved configuration to remove the attacker","Apply the mitigation, keep appliance evidence, hunt downstream, and reset credentials that passed through it","Replace the appliance with another vendor's product that is not affected by this vulnerability"],"a":[2],"e":"With no patch, the right path is to reduce exposure with the vendor mitigation, keep evidence, look for follow-on activity in the network, and reset credentials that passed through the device. Shutting down hits 8,000 users and may not be needed. A factory reset destroys evidence and may not remove persistence. A vendor swap is a later decision, not incident response."},
{"id":"o63","d":3,"q":"A payments web app handles about 50,000 requests per second. Logging budget allows only selected events to be sent to the SIEM, and PCI DSS applies. Developers currently log full request bodies at debug level in a few services. The app runs on Kubernetes with a service mesh, and the SIEM contract renews next spring. Which log events are MOST important to send for security monitoring?","o":["Full request and response bodies for transactions, so investigators can replay activity","All HTTP access logs at debug detail, including headers, from each service and the mesh","Authentication and authorization events, input failures, privilege changes, and sensitive data access","Only server errors and unhandled exceptions, since attacks often cause abnormal app behavior"],"a":[2],"e":"Authentication, authorization, input validation failures, privilege changes, and sensitive data access, with user and session context, cover most attacks at manageable volume. Full bodies add PII and cardholder data to logs and cost. Debug access logs are too much for the budget. Errors alone miss successful abuse."},
{"id":"o65","d":3,"q":"A lab instrument controller runs Windows 7 with FDA-validated software that cannot be changed without revalidation. Replacement is planned in 18 months. The controller exports results to a file share nightly. The instrument vendor says EDR agents are not supported. The lab manager also asked for a new printer on that network. Which approach is BEST?","o":["Buy extended security updates for Windows 7 from Microsoft to keep it patched until replaced","Install a lightweight EDR agent anyway and accept the support risk for the validated system","Air-gap the controller and move results by USB drive so it has no network exposure at all","Segment it, allow only the export flow, monitor, and formally accept the risk until replacement"],"a":[3],"e":"Isolation, allowing only the export flow, monitoring, and time-bound risk acceptance tied to the replacement date reduce exposure without breaking validation. Windows 7 extended updates have ended. An unsupported agent may break the validated state. USB transfer adds a malware path and manual work."},
{"id":"o66","d":3,"q":"A global logistics company has three regional SOCs with different SIEMs, playbooks, and severity definitions. During a recent multi-region incident, each SOC rated the same activity differently and nobody coordinated for 18 hours. A SIEM consolidation is proposed for two years from now. The regions are in different time zones and languages. What is the BEST first improvement?","o":["Consolidate onto a single SIEM so the three SOCs share one data set and one common rule base","Merge the three regional SOCs into one global SOC with a single management chain","Hold a daily sync call between regional SOC leads to compare open incidents and ratings","Adopt a common taxonomy and severity model, a global incident commander, and shared cases"],"a":[3],"e":"The failure was in shared language and coordination, so a common taxonomy and severity model, a defined global escalation path, and shared case management fix it quickly. A single SIEM is a long project and does not fix process. Merging SOCs is a big change and loses regional coverage. A daily call is too slow for an active incident."},
{"id":"o68","d":3,"q":"A company wants to use canary files to catch ransomware on its file servers. The servers hold about 40 TB across thousands of shares. Backup jobs and antivirus scan the shares nightly. A pilot placed obvious decoy files and got false alerts every night. The SOC has SOAR with EDR integration. Ransomware groups seen in the sector usually encrypt shares in directory order. Which design is MOST effective?","o":["Put a single hidden folder of canaries at the root of each server to keep users away from them","Mark the canary files read-only so that ransomware cannot encrypt them and they stay intact","Name the files clearly as canaries so that staff know not to open, move, or change them","Seed realistic files where ransomware hits first, exclude scanners, auto-contain on change"],"a":[3],"e":"Canaries work when they look real, sit where ransomware enumerates early, are excluded from legitimate scanners that cause false alerts, and trigger automated containment. One hidden folder may be hit late. Read-only files may be skipped or still give no signal. Clearly labeled canaries can be spotted and avoided by attackers."},
{"id":"o70","d":3,"q":"A SOC collects from about 150 sources, each with its own parser and field names. Analysts write a separate query for each firewall and EDR vendor, and cross-source hunting is slow. The company is adding a security data lake alongside its SIEM. Some vendors support CEF output, many do not. The SOC is also hiring two engineers. Which approach is MOST useful?","o":["Require all sources to send logs in CEF and drop sources that cannot meet the requirement","Keep raw formats and build query-time views per source for analysts to use","Normalize to a common schema like OCSF in the pipeline, keeping the raw events as well","Store only raw logs in the data lake and rely on natural-language search to find events"],"a":[2],"e":"Normalizing to an open schema like OCSF or ECS in the pipeline gives consistent fields across the SIEM and data lake, and keeping raw data supports forensics and reparsing. Forcing CEF drops useful sources. Query-time views per source keep the per-vendor work analysts already struggle with. Raw-only storage leaves normalization unsolved."},
{"id":"o71","d":3,"q":"An e-commerce company is under a volumetric DDoS that its scrubbing provider is handling. At the same time, the SOC sees successful logins to the admin portal from new IPs using two service accounts. Most of the IR team is on the DDoS bridge call. The admin portal sits behind the same CDN. The CEO is asking for hourly updates on the outage. What should the IR lead do?","o":["Focus the team on the DDoS until service is restored, and then review the admin portal logins","Block all access to the admin portal until the DDoS ends and then investigate the logins","Geo-block the regions where the new admin IPs come from and keep the team on the DDoS","Treat the DDoS as a possible diversion and assign separate staff to the logins right away"],"a":[3],"e":"Attackers use DDoS to distract responders. Successful logins with service accounts may be the real attack, so they need separate, immediate investigation while the provider handles the DDoS. Waiting gives the attacker time. Blocking the portal outright may disrupt operations and does not answer what happened. Geo-blocking is easy for attackers to dodge."},
{"id":"o74","d":3,"q":"A company wants better detection of lateral movement in its Windows domain. Budget allows one major data source improvement this year. Today the SIEM gets firewall logs and domain controller security logs, but nothing from member servers or workstations. Attackers in a past incident used PsExec and WMI. The company also runs a NetFlow collector. Which data source is MOST valuable to add?","o":["NetFlow from the core switches, with alerts on new SMB and RPC flows between hosts","More detailed domain controller logging, including all Kerberos ticket requests","Endpoint process creation with command lines, remote logons, and EDR telemetry","Firewall logs from the internal segmentation firewalls between server VLANs"],"a":[2],"e":"Lateral movement shows on the hosts: remote logons and process creation with command lines reveal PsExec, WMI, and similar tools. NetFlow shows connections but not what ran. DC logs see authentication but miss execution. Internal firewall logs show allowed flows, not actions."},
{"id":"o76","d":3,"q":"A company uses one MSSP for all monitoring. The MSSP has standing admin access to the EDR console and firewall managers through shared accounts, plus a site-to-site VPN into the management network. This morning the MSSP disclosed a breach of its own environment, with few details. The contract runs two more years. The company's internal SOC has two analysts. What should the company do FIRST?","o":["Give notice to end the contract and begin moving monitoring to another provider","Wait for the MSSP's full breach report so that actions are based on confirmed facts","Suspend MSSP access paths, rotate the shared credentials, and review MSSP-originated actions in the logs","Bring all monitoring in-house right away using the two internal analysts and existing tooling"],"a":[2],"e":"The immediate risk is the MSSP's trusted access being used against the company, so cut those paths, rotate shared credentials, and check what was done through them. Ending the contract is a later decision. Waiting for a report leaves access open. Two analysts cannot take over full monitoring overnight, and it does not close the access risk."},
{"id":"o77","d":3,"q":"Attackers used a malicious OAuth app connected to the company's Salesforce org to pull customer records. The IR team found that detailed event logs were not available because the Event Monitoring add-on was never licensed, and nobody knew how to reach Salesforce security. The IR plan covers on-premises and IaaS only. The company uses SSO for Salesforce. What should the updated plan include for SaaS incidents?","o":["A contract clause requiring each SaaS provider to run incident response on the company's own behalf","Provider escalation contacts, known logs and response actions, and log export set up in advance","An API-mode CASB connected to major SaaS apps to scan for sensitive data and risky sharing","Reliance on SSO and IdP sign-in logs as the main evidence source for SaaS investigations"],"a":[1],"e":"Under shared responsibility, the customer must know how to reach the provider, what logs and actions exist (and license them), and export logs before an incident. Providers rarely run customer IR. A CASB helps posture and detection but is not an IR plan. SSO logs miss OAuth app activity, which bypasses interactive sign-in."},
{"id":"o78","d":3,"q":"A company's cloud bill rose 40% in a week. It runs Kubernetes clusters with autoscaling and a few GPU instance families for an ML team. GuardDuty is enabled. The finance team already gets billing alerts, but those arrive days after usage. The company also runs a cloud cost optimization tool, and the ML team often launches large jobs without notice. Which signal is MOST indicative of cryptojacking?","o":["Billing anomaly alerts showing spend in the compute category running well above the forecast","High sustained CPU on cluster nodes, since mining is compute-heavy work by design","New compute in unused regions or types, sustained load, and connections to mining pools","An increase in failed API calls from service accounts across the cloud accounts"],"a":[2],"e":"Mining shows as unexpected compute, often in unused regions or GPU types, running hot and talking to mining pools, which GuardDuty and flow logs can show. Billing alerts lag by days. High CPU alone is normal with autoscaling and ML work. Failed API calls may show recon but not mining."},
{"id":"o85","d":3,"q":"EDR shows an active command-and-control beacon on the CFO's laptop. The CFO is traveling and refuses isolation before a board presentation in three hours. Policy says the SOC should isolate high-severity infections, but the CFO outranks the CISO. The laptop holds draft earnings data. The help desk can courier a loaner by tomorrow. What should the incident handler do?","o":["Isolate the laptop now as policy requires and inform the CFO of the reason afterward","Wait until after the presentation, then isolate, while watching the beacon closely","Escalate to the CISO and CEO with options, like blocking C2 or a clean device","Let the CFO's executive assistant decide the timing based on the CFO's calendar"],"a":[2],"e":"An executive refusing containment is a risk decision above the handler's authority, so escalate fast with clear options like blocking the C2 domain or giving a clean device for the presentation. Isolating against a senior executive without escalation may be overruled and damage trust. Waiting three hours with active C2 risks earnings data. An assistant has no authority to accept the risk."},
{"id":"o86","d":3,"q":"Production backups use the cloud provider's native service and sit in vaults in the production account. They are encrypted with a customer-managed key that also lives in the production account. Several admins have broad rights there. The provider supports vault lock in compliance mode and cross-account copies. The company plans a migration to a new region next year. Which improvement BEST protects backups against account compromise?","o":["Turn on vault lock in compliance mode for the vaults inside the production account","Copy backups into a locked vault in another account using keys that account owns","Add an IAM policy denying backup deletion to all roles except the backup admin role","Require MFA for backup deletion on the vaults and alert on deletion attempts by admins"],"a":[1],"e":"Even locked backups are useless if an attacker in the account can schedule deletion of the encryption key. A separate account with its own keys and a locked vault protects against both deletion and key loss. Same-account vault lock leaves the key exposed. IAM deny policies and MFA can be changed by an admin who controls the account."},
{"id":"o88","d":3,"q":"IR playbooks assume the SOC can reach laptops over the VPN to collect data and push fixes. Most staff now work remotely and use the VPN only for a few legacy apps. During a recent incident, the SOC could not reach the affected laptop for two days. Devices run cloud-managed EDR and Intune. Some users are in countries with poor connectivity. What should be updated?","o":["Require a persistent full-tunnel VPN on laptops so the existing playbooks work as they were written","Add steps to have the user ship the laptop to IT when remote access is not possible","Rewrite playbooks around EDR, Intune, and identity actions that work over the internet","Add a step for the help desk to call the user and talk them through manual collection"],"a":[2],"e":"The tools already in place work over any internet connection, so the playbooks should use EDR collection and containment, MDM actions, and identity controls. Always-on VPN is possible but adds cost and friction just to fit old playbooks, and struggles on poor links. Shipping laptops takes days. Talking users through collection is slow and unreliable."},
{"id":"o90","d":3,"q":"A company rolled out an approved enterprise AI assistant with agents that can call internal APIs to create tickets and query HR data. Usage logs show some staff still paste customer data into consumer chatbots. The security team has a secure web gateway with DLP and an API gateway in front of internal services. Legal is drafting an AI use policy. Which risk is MOST important to monitor?","o":["Harmful or biased output from the approved assistant, flagged through user feedback","License and seat usage of the assistant to confirm adoption against the business case","Data sent to unapproved AI services, and agent API calls that break from baseline","Prompt volume per user, to find staff relying on AI tools more than expected"],"a":[2],"e":"The biggest security risks are sensitive data leaving through unapproved services and approved agents being abused to act on internal systems, both visible through DLP and API logs. Output quality matters but is not the main security concern. License and volume tracking are adoption metrics."}
);
